WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:08.720 --> 00:00:10.100
- Hello.

00:00:10.100 --> 00:00:11.850
Good afternoon, everyone.

00:00:11.850 --> 00:00:13.460
My name is Priya Natarajan,

00:00:13.460 --> 00:00:15.470
I'm an astrophysicist and faculty member

00:00:15.470 --> 00:00:18.020
in the Departments of
Astronomy and Physics at Yale.

00:00:18.020 --> 00:00:20.160
I also serve currently as the director

00:00:20.160 --> 00:00:23.360
of the Franke Program in
Science and the Humanities.

00:00:23.360 --> 00:00:25.660
I'm absolutely delighted
to welcome you all

00:00:25.660 --> 00:00:28.390
to the Franke Distinguished
Lecture Series.

00:00:28.390 --> 00:00:30.140
I would first like to thank the donors,

00:00:30.140 --> 00:00:31.810
Richard and Barbara Franke,

00:00:31.810 --> 00:00:34.780
whose generous support
has made this possible.

00:00:34.780 --> 00:00:38.120
Before I introduce today's star speaker,

00:00:38.120 --> 00:00:40.900
it's been a real coup for
us to be able to get him,

00:00:40.900 --> 00:00:43.100
to host him and hear him today.

00:00:43.100 --> 00:00:45.440
I need to make a few
practical announcements.

00:00:45.440 --> 00:00:48.440
I'm required to give you
all notice that this event

00:00:48.440 --> 00:00:51.500
is being recorded and
photographed for educational

00:00:51.500 --> 00:00:54.730
archival and promotional
purposes, including use in print

00:00:54.730 --> 00:00:56.770
on the internet and other forms of media.

00:00:56.770 --> 00:00:59.650
By attending this event today
you agree to the possibility

00:00:59.650 --> 00:01:02.390
of your voice or likeness
captured by these means

00:01:02.390 --> 00:01:05.280
and used for such purposes
without compensation to you

00:01:05.280 --> 00:01:09.810
and hereby wave any related
right of privacy or publicity.

00:01:09.810 --> 00:01:12.370
And I also want to announce,
continuing first in the

00:01:12.370 --> 00:01:15.550
practical lane that immediately
following the lecture,

00:01:15.550 --> 00:01:18.810
please do join us for
a reception in room 108

00:01:18.810 --> 00:01:21.670
to celebrate and to
continue the conversation.

00:01:21.670 --> 00:01:23.930
Now to today's speaker, Steven Strogatz.

00:01:23.930 --> 00:01:26.210
Steve is a Jacob Gould Schurman Professor

00:01:26.210 --> 00:01:28.620
of Applied Mathematics
at Cornell University.

00:01:28.620 --> 00:01:31.210
After graduating summa cum
laude in mathematics from

00:01:31.210 --> 00:01:35.090
Princeton in 1980, Steve
studied at Trinity College

00:01:35.090 --> 00:01:38.440
Cambridge, my alma mater,
where he has a Marshall Scholar.

00:01:38.440 --> 00:01:41.600
He did his doctoral work in
applied mathematics at Harvard,

00:01:41.600 --> 00:01:43.320
followed by a National Science Foundation

00:01:43.320 --> 00:01:47.080
post-doctoral fellowship, also at Harvard.

00:01:47.080 --> 00:01:51.210
From 1989 to 1994, he
taught at MIT before joining

00:01:51.210 --> 00:01:55.870
the Cornell faculty in 1994,
where he has been since.

00:01:55.870 --> 00:01:58.090
What is really remarkable about Steve

00:01:58.090 --> 00:02:01.440
is the originality that
he brings to his broad set

00:02:01.440 --> 00:02:03.640
of research interests and mathematics.

00:02:03.640 --> 00:02:05.870
Early in his career he worked on a variety

00:02:05.870 --> 00:02:08.370
of problems in mathematical biology,

00:02:08.370 --> 00:02:10.690
which was a brand new field at the time.

00:02:10.690 --> 00:02:13.180
Studying the geometry of supercoil DNA,

00:02:13.180 --> 00:02:16.170
the dynamics of the
human sleep/wake cycle,

00:02:16.170 --> 00:02:18.740
the topology of three
dimensional chemical waves

00:02:18.740 --> 00:02:21.780
and the collective behavior
of biological oscillators,

00:02:21.780 --> 00:02:25.110
such as swarms of synchronously
flashing fireflies.

00:02:25.110 --> 00:02:29.630
In the 1990's, his work focused
more into another exciting

00:02:29.630 --> 00:02:31.190
and challenging area of mathematics,

00:02:31.190 --> 00:02:34.970
nonlinear dynamics and
chaos, as applied to physics,

00:02:34.970 --> 00:02:37.070
engineering and biology.

00:02:37.070 --> 00:02:41.140
And many of you probably
have used his textbook

00:02:41.140 --> 00:02:42.690
at some point or the other.

00:02:42.690 --> 00:02:45.340
Several of these projects
of his dealt with

00:02:45.340 --> 00:02:48.740
a couple dossiers such lasers,
superconducting Josephson

00:02:48.740 --> 00:02:52.110
junctions and crickets
that chirp in unison.

00:02:52.110 --> 00:02:54.700
And I have juxtaposed these
things just to show you what

00:02:54.700 --> 00:02:58.120
the range of the systems to which

00:02:58.120 --> 00:03:02.290
he has been applying
mathematical structures to.

00:03:02.290 --> 00:03:04.660
In each case, his research involved close

00:03:04.660 --> 00:03:06.760
collaborations with experimentalists.

00:03:06.760 --> 00:03:10.370
He also likes boldly
branching out into new areas,

00:03:10.370 --> 00:03:12.060
often with students taking the lead.

00:03:12.060 --> 00:03:15.080
In the past few years this
has lead him to such topics,

00:03:15.080 --> 00:03:19.600
fun topics, as the role of
crowd synchronization in the

00:03:19.600 --> 00:03:24.130
wobbling of London's Millennium
Bridge on its opening day,

00:03:24.130 --> 00:03:28.040
and the dynamics of structural
balance in social systems.

00:03:28.040 --> 00:03:30.020
One of his seminal research contributions

00:03:30.020 --> 00:03:34.960
is a landmark 1998 nature
paper on small world networks,

00:03:34.960 --> 00:03:37.890
that was coauthored with his
former student, Duncan Watts.

00:03:37.890 --> 00:03:40.560
This is considered to be one
of the most influential papers

00:03:40.560 --> 00:03:43.097
in network science and was, you know,

00:03:43.097 --> 00:03:46.080
it was the most highly
sited paper on networks

00:03:46.080 --> 00:03:49.520
for decades as well as
the sixth most highly

00:03:49.520 --> 00:03:53.580
sited paper on any topic in physics.

00:03:53.580 --> 00:03:57.610
It has now been sited
more than 38,000 times

00:03:57.610 --> 00:03:59.053
according to Google Scholar.

00:04:00.420 --> 00:04:03.020
Steve has received numerous
awards for his research,

00:04:03.020 --> 00:04:05.750
his teaching and his public communication.

00:04:05.750 --> 00:04:09.160
And enumerating all of them
would take up too much time.

00:04:09.160 --> 00:04:12.480
So I will just mention
two most recent awards.

00:04:12.480 --> 00:04:15.700
The Lewis Thomas Prize
for Writing about Science,

00:04:15.700 --> 00:04:18.960
which honors the scientist as poet and the

00:04:18.960 --> 00:04:22.780
SIAM George Polya Prize for
Mathematical Exposition.

00:04:22.780 --> 00:04:25.360
So one of the unique things
about Steve in addition

00:04:25.360 --> 00:04:29.180
to his research work in
mathematics, he's deeply devoted to

00:04:29.180 --> 00:04:32.310
the public dissemination of mathematics.

00:04:32.310 --> 00:04:35.210
He has been elected to many,
many prestigious societies

00:04:35.210 --> 00:04:38.410
for both of these kinds of
work, both his research work

00:04:38.410 --> 00:04:42.670
and the work he does in
popularizing and making

00:04:42.670 --> 00:04:45.010
increasing mathematical literacy.

00:04:45.010 --> 00:04:47.580
He's a fellow of the Society of Industrial

00:04:47.580 --> 00:04:49.760
and Applied Mathematics,
the American Academy of Arts

00:04:49.760 --> 00:04:51.630
and Sciences, the
American Physical Society

00:04:51.630 --> 00:04:53.610
and the American Mathematical Society,

00:04:53.610 --> 00:04:56.890
and of course he's spoken
at all the shi-shi venues,

00:04:56.890 --> 00:04:59.070
TED, Aspen Ideas Festival,

00:04:59.070 --> 00:05:01.120
et cetera, et cetera and
he's a frequent guest

00:05:01.120 --> 00:05:03.680
on Radio Lab and Science Friday.

00:05:03.680 --> 00:05:07.740
And he is the author of
Nonlinear Dynamics and Chaos,

00:05:07.740 --> 00:05:09.280
which is a 1994 textbook,

00:05:09.280 --> 00:05:12.830
which is the standard in the subject,

00:05:12.830 --> 00:05:17.650
and in 2009, starting in
2003 he started writing much

00:05:17.650 --> 00:05:21.430
more for the public as
well as his research work.

00:05:21.430 --> 00:05:23.900
In 2003, he wrote a book called Sync,

00:05:23.900 --> 00:05:28.880
2009 the Calculus of Friendship
and 2012 book that most

00:05:28.880 --> 00:05:32.300
of you have probably
heard about, The Joy of X,

00:05:32.300 --> 00:05:33.980
which incidentally has been translated

00:05:33.980 --> 00:05:36.220
into about 15 languages.

00:05:36.220 --> 00:05:38.810
His current book, Infinite Powers,

00:05:38.810 --> 00:05:41.083
will form the basis of his talk today.

00:05:41.920 --> 00:05:43.510
So in closing, I wanna quote

00:05:43.510 --> 00:05:45.917
the mathematician Georg Cantor who said

00:05:45.917 --> 00:05:48.357
"Mathematicians do not
study pure mathematics

00:05:48.357 --> 00:05:49.787
"because it's useful.

00:05:49.787 --> 00:05:52.047
"They study it because it delights them

00:05:52.047 --> 00:05:55.050
"and they delight in it
because it's beautiful."

00:05:55.050 --> 00:05:57.500
So without further ado, it's my pleasure

00:05:57.500 --> 00:06:00.100
to invite Steven Strogatz,
who will be speaking to us

00:06:00.100 --> 00:06:03.170
today about the beauty of calculus.

00:06:03.170 --> 00:06:04.186
(audience applauding)
- Thank you.

00:06:04.186 --> 00:06:05.950
- [Priya] Hi there, thanks for coming.

00:06:05.950 --> 00:06:06.816
- Thanks.

00:06:06.816 --> 00:06:07.649
- [Priya] Are you comfortable?

00:06:07.649 --> 00:06:08.982
- Yeah, I think.

00:06:14.000 --> 00:06:16.810
Welcome, thanks for coming.

00:06:16.810 --> 00:06:18.110
I'm very delighted to be here.

00:06:18.110 --> 00:06:21.990
I just drove down from
Ithaca and it felt good

00:06:21.990 --> 00:06:23.100
to be back in Connecticut.

00:06:23.100 --> 00:06:26.300
I'm a Connecticut boy,
grew up in Torrington.

00:06:26.300 --> 00:06:27.750
Just go right up Route 8.

00:06:29.960 --> 00:06:31.450
It's an interesting choice of quote,

00:06:31.450 --> 00:06:33.260
you mentioning Cantor's quote,

00:06:33.260 --> 00:06:34.910
because I'm an applied mathematician.

00:06:34.910 --> 00:06:36.820
So I don't know that I
really agree with Cantor.

00:06:36.820 --> 00:06:39.143
I do delight in the
usefulness of mathematics.

00:06:39.143 --> 00:06:40.240
(audience laughing)

00:06:40.240 --> 00:06:42.020
But I also certainly
delight in the beauty,

00:06:42.020 --> 00:06:43.333
like all mathematicians.

00:06:44.170 --> 00:06:47.160
We have the exciting
feature today that I don't

00:06:47.160 --> 00:06:49.120
have a a computer in front of me

00:06:49.120 --> 00:06:51.483
and we're gonna do this old school.

00:06:52.740 --> 00:06:56.048
The Dean of Science is here to
help me go through my slides.

00:06:56.048 --> 00:06:57.290
(chuckling)

00:06:57.290 --> 00:07:00.100
So I will, I mean it may
seem a little bit strange,

00:07:00.100 --> 00:07:02.100
but I'm gonna be saying next, next, next

00:07:02.100 --> 00:07:04.230
and he'll, he's gonna toggle through.

00:07:04.230 --> 00:07:07.210
We think this will work, so
bear with us if it doesn't.

00:07:07.210 --> 00:07:08.310
But hopefully it will.

00:07:09.290 --> 00:07:10.655
We have not rehearsed.

00:07:10.655 --> 00:07:11.950
(chuckling)
(audience laughing)

00:07:11.950 --> 00:07:13.410
Thank you Jeff for agreeing to do it.

00:07:13.410 --> 00:07:14.540
Okay, next.

00:07:14.540 --> 00:07:15.373
Should I say next?

00:07:15.373 --> 00:07:16.970
What's the coolest thing to say?

00:07:16.970 --> 00:07:17.910
Next.

00:07:17.910 --> 00:07:19.350
Okay, yeah next.

00:07:19.350 --> 00:07:20.666
All right, no, don't do next, well--

00:07:20.666 --> 00:07:23.200
(audience laughing)

00:07:23.200 --> 00:07:26.212
So yeah, on this theme
of calculus being useful,

00:07:26.212 --> 00:07:30.730
it's an unsung hero of our existence.

00:07:30.730 --> 00:07:33.240
It's everywhere and
you may not realize it,

00:07:33.240 --> 00:07:36.493
but without calculus
we wouldn't have radio,

00:07:38.750 --> 00:07:43.470
television, microwave ovens,
yeah, you can kind of go

00:07:43.470 --> 00:07:45.640
through them fairly quickly
because these are all just,

00:07:45.640 --> 00:07:47.780
we wouldn't have been able to
put astronauts on the moon,

00:07:47.780 --> 00:07:50.860
unravel the human genome, we
wouldn't have nuclear energy

00:07:50.860 --> 00:07:54.270
or nuclear weapons, we
wouldn't have ultrasound

00:07:54.270 --> 00:07:58.110
for expectant mothers or
GPS for lost travelers

00:07:58.110 --> 00:08:02.540
and we might not even have the
Declaration of Independence.

00:08:02.540 --> 00:08:05.090
Now, some of these I
realize may seem farfetched,

00:08:05.090 --> 00:08:07.530
I'd be happy to talk at
the end if you want me

00:08:07.530 --> 00:08:09.393
to try to defend any of these.

00:08:10.250 --> 00:08:12.330
Some of the connections are
tenuous, but I think I could

00:08:12.330 --> 00:08:14.550
make a case for everything
I've just said there.

00:08:14.550 --> 00:08:17.460
So it's invisible, I mean most of us have

00:08:17.460 --> 00:08:20.130
no idea that calculus is a part of all

00:08:20.130 --> 00:08:22.530
of these things and many
more, but it really is.

00:08:23.550 --> 00:08:24.383
Next.

00:08:25.220 --> 00:08:27.660
So the take home message from that

00:08:27.660 --> 00:08:30.310
is that calculus helped
make the world modern.

00:08:30.310 --> 00:08:32.010
Now of course, it didn't do it on its own.

00:08:32.010 --> 00:08:34.470
It wasn't calculus that
put people on the moon.

00:08:34.470 --> 00:08:37.990
You needed engineers, you
need physicists, so it's a,

00:08:37.990 --> 00:08:42.080
think of it as like in a drama, you know,

00:08:42.080 --> 00:08:44.430
in a theater there are supporting players.

00:08:44.430 --> 00:08:46.820
This is, calculus is sort
of a supporting player that

00:08:46.820 --> 00:08:48.810
you've seen in every movie or every play

00:08:48.810 --> 00:08:50.900
and you never really
noticed that it was there,

00:08:50.900 --> 00:08:53.630
but it's a very important
player in the drama.

00:08:53.630 --> 00:08:54.463
Next.

00:08:55.420 --> 00:08:58.830
Now, the question is, at
least the first question,

00:08:58.830 --> 00:09:02.760
here's a branch of math that
many people find sort of arcane

00:09:02.760 --> 00:09:05.830
and abstruse, certainly a lot
of freshman taking freshman

00:09:05.830 --> 00:09:08.860
calculus would say, I don't
see the point of calculus,

00:09:08.860 --> 00:09:10.610
both before they took the course

00:09:10.610 --> 00:09:11.961
and after they took the course.

00:09:11.961 --> 00:09:13.390
(audience laughing)
(chuckling)

00:09:13.390 --> 00:09:16.190
And a lot of the students taking
advanced placement calculus

00:09:16.190 --> 00:09:19.230
and I dare say many of
their teachers would be

00:09:19.230 --> 00:09:21.870
hard pressed to say, why
are we doing this exactly?

00:09:21.870 --> 00:09:25.180
You know, calculus is in such
a big rush, there's so much to

00:09:25.180 --> 00:09:28.960
cover to get ready for the
advanced placement test or for

00:09:28.960 --> 00:09:31.520
whatever, there's so
many techniques to learn,

00:09:31.520 --> 00:09:35.270
so many theorems to be understood,
so much jargon to absorb

00:09:35.270 --> 00:09:40.040
that the larger context is
often excluded or just not even,

00:09:40.040 --> 00:09:42.270
you know, no one would even
think of including the history,

00:09:42.270 --> 00:09:45.100
the applications, the
philosophy, the connections to

00:09:45.100 --> 00:09:48.370
medicine and physics and
sociology and everything else.

00:09:48.370 --> 00:09:52.020
But I wanna try to give you
the big picture of calculus,

00:09:52.020 --> 00:09:53.750
and I don't assume that you've had it

00:09:53.750 --> 00:09:55.380
or that you even know what it is.

00:09:55.380 --> 00:09:57.050
Now, it could be that, you know,

00:09:57.050 --> 00:09:58.500
I don't know, you may
be ringers out there.

00:09:58.500 --> 00:10:01.180
Maybe all of you are mathematicians,

00:10:01.180 --> 00:10:02.770
but I don't assume that.

00:10:02.770 --> 00:10:05.000
This lecture series as you may know,

00:10:05.000 --> 00:10:07.040
is supposed to be a meeting ground

00:10:07.040 --> 00:10:10.010
for the humanities and the sciences,

00:10:10.010 --> 00:10:13.090
and I'm very grateful to
the Franke's for you know,

00:10:13.090 --> 00:10:16.580
for sponsoring this colloquium series.

00:10:16.580 --> 00:10:19.520
It's fantastic to bring the
humanists and the scientists

00:10:19.520 --> 00:10:21.080
and the mathematicians together.

00:10:21.080 --> 00:10:23.840
So in that spirit I'm gonna
be avoiding for the most part,

00:10:23.840 --> 00:10:26.400
technical math, because it's
really not very realistic to

00:10:26.400 --> 00:10:30.190
do that if you've never thought
about these things before.

00:10:30.190 --> 00:10:32.040
But I do wanna give you the flavor of some

00:10:32.040 --> 00:10:33.990
of the gorgeous arguments and reasoning

00:10:33.990 --> 00:10:37.433
and the stories of why
calculus is so important.

00:10:38.270 --> 00:10:40.510
So, the question I'm
asking there is you know,

00:10:40.510 --> 00:10:43.520
here's this arcane branch of
math, how could it be that it

00:10:43.520 --> 00:10:47.220
helped to reshape civilization,
and I'm making a little word

00:10:47.220 --> 00:10:49.170
game there because it
grew out of geometry.

00:10:49.170 --> 00:10:52.550
It was originally about shapes
and it reshaped the world.

00:10:52.550 --> 00:10:53.503
Okay, next.

00:10:54.540 --> 00:10:58.030
So in this spirit of humanities
trying to find common ground

00:10:58.030 --> 00:11:00.380
with the sciences and with
math, I wanna tell you a little

00:11:00.380 --> 00:11:05.380
story first of two men who
met I'd say it was probably

00:11:06.920 --> 00:11:09.830
the late 1940s, early 1950s.

00:11:09.830 --> 00:11:12.370
So my parents would have
recognized this gentleman,

00:11:12.370 --> 00:11:14.890
maybe a lot of you will
not know who he is.

00:11:14.890 --> 00:11:18.630
Herman Wouk was a great novelist.

00:11:18.630 --> 00:11:21.113
He's actually still
alive, he's 103 years old.

00:11:22.200 --> 00:11:25.010
But in my parent's generation
he was the chronicler

00:11:25.010 --> 00:11:28.790
of World War II for a mass audience.

00:11:28.790 --> 00:11:31.500
And so he wrote these
famous books, Winds of War,

00:11:31.500 --> 00:11:33.323
War and Remembrance novels.

00:11:34.200 --> 00:11:36.010
You probably have heard
of the Caine Mutiny,

00:11:36.010 --> 00:11:38.430
Humphrey Bogart was in the movie.

00:11:38.430 --> 00:11:40.530
That's his first, I think
it was maybe his first book,

00:11:40.530 --> 00:11:41.983
Pulitzer Prize winning book.

00:11:44.310 --> 00:11:48.220
So Wouk wanted to write a
book about World War II.

00:11:48.220 --> 00:11:50.220
This was before, I mean that book,

00:11:50.220 --> 00:11:52.380
the book he had in mind
grew into two big books.

00:11:52.380 --> 00:11:54.980
Those two books, Winds of
War and War and Remembrance.

00:11:54.980 --> 00:11:57.180
But at the time he had this
vision of I'm gonna write

00:11:57.180 --> 00:11:58.730
a great novel about World War II.

00:11:58.730 --> 00:12:01.530
He was in the war himself as a sailor,

00:12:01.530 --> 00:12:06.530
and he wanted in particular to
interview some scientists who

00:12:06.620 --> 00:12:09.500
had worked on the Manhattan
Project, on the building

00:12:09.500 --> 00:12:12.460
of the atomic bomb, and he was told that

00:12:12.460 --> 00:12:14.870
you could find people
like this at Caltech.

00:12:14.870 --> 00:12:16.707
And if you go to Caltech,
there's someone that

00:12:16.707 --> 00:12:21.253
you should meet, but he's
difficult named Richard Feynman.

00:12:22.530 --> 00:12:24.290
So, let's have the next slide.

00:12:24.290 --> 00:12:25.490
There's Richard Feynman.

00:12:27.030 --> 00:12:28.680
You see him there in a classroom.

00:12:28.680 --> 00:12:32.010
Those are the days when
it was all boys probably.

00:12:32.010 --> 00:12:35.040
Caltech is still quite,
I think isn't it pretty

00:12:35.040 --> 00:12:36.270
bad gender ratio?

00:12:36.270 --> 00:12:38.780
I think so, but anyway, in
those days probably 100%.

00:12:38.780 --> 00:12:40.170
But you know, he was a joker.

00:12:40.170 --> 00:12:41.690
You see him there playing the bongos,

00:12:41.690 --> 00:12:44.643
he used to break into safes at Los Alamos.

00:12:46.248 --> 00:12:50.490
He's written many books,
chronicling his escapades.

00:12:50.490 --> 00:12:53.100
He loved to tell stories about himself.

00:12:53.100 --> 00:12:54.270
So he's a joker.

00:12:54.270 --> 00:12:56.070
He was also a great teacher and had

00:12:57.940 --> 00:13:00.620
a very deep understanding of
physics and won a Nobel Prize

00:13:00.620 --> 00:13:03.620
for his work in an area called
quantum electrodynamics,

00:13:03.620 --> 00:13:05.160
but anyways, so he is sitting there,

00:13:05.160 --> 00:13:07.440
when he had worked on
the Manhattan Project

00:13:07.440 --> 00:13:10.370
he was very young, like maybe early 20's.

00:13:10.370 --> 00:13:14.110
So Wouk comes to see him,
they start talking, they talk,

00:13:14.110 --> 00:13:15.910
they have a lot in common it turns out.

00:13:15.910 --> 00:13:18.500
They're about the same
age, they're both Jewish,

00:13:18.500 --> 00:13:20.600
there's a difference you
know, Feynman is quite

00:13:20.600 --> 00:13:24.340
an extreme atheist, Wouk is very devout

00:13:24.340 --> 00:13:26.990
and so they get to talking,
they like each other

00:13:26.990 --> 00:13:28.700
and they're having fun back and forth,

00:13:28.700 --> 00:13:32.430
talking about things, arguing
about science and religion,

00:13:32.430 --> 00:13:35.420
and then after they leave,
just as Wouk is leaving,

00:13:35.420 --> 00:13:39.830
walking out the door Feynman
says I have a question for you.

00:13:39.830 --> 00:13:41.930
Do you know calculus?

00:13:41.930 --> 00:13:45.670
And Wouk admits you know, no I don't.

00:13:45.670 --> 00:13:47.600
And Feynman says well
you had better learn it,

00:13:47.600 --> 00:13:49.841
it's the language God talks.

00:13:49.841 --> 00:13:53.400
(audience laughing)

00:13:53.400 --> 00:13:55.670
So I wanna leave you with
that thought right now.

00:13:55.670 --> 00:13:56.910
The language God talks.

00:13:56.910 --> 00:13:57.743
Next slide.

00:13:59.000 --> 00:14:01.700
That's a book then, that became
the title of Herman Wouk's

00:14:01.700 --> 00:14:03.350
book about science and religion,

00:14:03.350 --> 00:14:07.840
sparked by his confrontation
with, I mean if you wanna

00:14:07.840 --> 00:14:10.340
call it a confrontation,
his meeting with Feynman.

00:14:13.060 --> 00:14:14.020
And it's a terrific book.

00:14:14.020 --> 00:14:16.400
It includes his conversations
with Steven Weinberg

00:14:16.400 --> 00:14:18.933
and various other well known physicists.

00:14:19.960 --> 00:14:20.810
I don't know if you can read it,

00:14:20.810 --> 00:14:22.830
but the subtitle is on
science and religion.

00:14:22.830 --> 00:14:25.370
So if you've ever wondered
about trying to reconcile

00:14:25.370 --> 00:14:28.500
your faith with science
or can they be reconciled?

00:14:28.500 --> 00:14:31.313
They certainly address a lot
of the same deep mysteries.

00:14:32.780 --> 00:14:34.900
This could be an interesting
book for you to look at.

00:14:34.900 --> 00:14:38.160
But anyway, so The Language
God Talks, now Wouk as I say,

00:14:38.160 --> 00:14:41.710
did not know calculus and he
tells a little story in the

00:14:41.710 --> 00:14:45.080
book about how you know, if
this is the language God talks,

00:14:45.080 --> 00:14:46.410
maybe he should learn it.

00:14:46.410 --> 00:14:48.960
So he describes his
efforts to go out and try

00:14:48.960 --> 00:14:51.313
to learn calculus and I take that as,

00:14:53.890 --> 00:14:55.630
sort of a metaphor for
what I think we're trying

00:14:55.630 --> 00:14:56.840
to do here today.

00:14:56.840 --> 00:14:59.110
That here's someone from the
humanities making a good faith

00:14:59.110 --> 00:15:02.450
effort to try to learn what
we have to offer in math.

00:15:02.450 --> 00:15:04.290
So next slide.

00:15:04.290 --> 00:15:07.620
Wouk tells this little
story of how, he says,

00:15:07.620 --> 00:15:10.780
he went to the bookstore,
he skimmed some freshman

00:15:10.780 --> 00:15:13.440
textbooks hoping to come
across one that might help

00:15:13.440 --> 00:15:16.870
a mathematical ignoramus like me who

00:15:16.870 --> 00:15:19.540
had spent his college
years in the humanities,

00:15:19.540 --> 00:15:22.390
i.e. literature and
philosophy in an adolescent

00:15:22.390 --> 00:15:24.290
quest for the meaning of existence.

00:15:24.290 --> 00:15:27.070
Little knowing that calculus,
which I had heard of as a

00:15:27.070 --> 00:15:32.070
difficult bore leading nowhere,
was the language God talks.

00:15:33.300 --> 00:15:35.790
So, he sets out to teach himself calculus.

00:15:35.790 --> 00:15:39.483
He tries reading books with
titles like Calculus Made Easy,

00:15:41.032 --> 00:15:44.060
he hires and Israeli
tutor to help him learn

00:15:44.060 --> 00:15:47.023
his Hebrew better at the
same time learning some math,

00:15:48.206 --> 00:15:49.490
none of this working.

00:15:49.490 --> 00:15:53.860
He goes to, finally in desperation
he goes to a high school

00:15:53.860 --> 00:15:57.540
and enrolls in the high school
calculus class and after

00:15:57.540 --> 00:16:00.700
a few weeks he falls behind
and gives up and leaves

00:16:00.700 --> 00:16:03.420
and as he's walking out
the students clap for him,

00:16:03.420 --> 00:16:06.540
and he says it's like, you
know, when there's a pitiful

00:16:06.540 --> 00:16:09.784
showbiz act, there is this
concept of sympathy applause.

00:16:09.784 --> 00:16:11.140
(audience laughing)
That's what he felt like.

00:16:11.140 --> 00:16:12.230
So he had this really hard,

00:16:12.230 --> 00:16:14.603
he could not manage to
learn calculus and so,

00:16:17.080 --> 00:16:19.780
I wanna dedicate my book to Herman Wouk.

00:16:19.780 --> 00:16:21.200
I've never met him, I don't know him.

00:16:21.200 --> 00:16:22.460
I sent him a copy.

00:16:22.460 --> 00:16:24.320
He's in a home somewhere.

00:16:24.320 --> 00:16:25.620
I mean, he's very, very old.

00:16:25.620 --> 00:16:27.010
His wife recently died.

00:16:27.010 --> 00:16:28.260
I mean I think it's probably very hard

00:16:28.260 --> 00:16:30.260
for him at 103 right now.

00:16:30.260 --> 00:16:31.650
But, next slide.

00:16:31.650 --> 00:16:36.130
So, yeah so, Infinite Powers
is my attempt to write

00:16:36.130 --> 00:16:38.520
for people like Herman
Wouk, in the humanities.

00:16:38.520 --> 00:16:40.783
What is the reason that anyone would think

00:16:40.783 --> 00:16:43.030
that calculus is the language God talks.

00:16:43.030 --> 00:16:45.560
Why should, so my wife would tease me

00:16:45.560 --> 00:16:46.450
for saying it like this.

00:16:46.450 --> 00:16:48.843
Why should a normal person learn calculus?

00:16:48.843 --> 00:16:51.630
(chuckling)

00:16:51.630 --> 00:16:53.300
And by the way, I don't feel necessarily

00:16:53.300 --> 00:16:54.840
you need to learn calculus.

00:16:54.840 --> 00:16:57.170
This is a story about
appreciating calculus.

00:16:57.170 --> 00:16:59.980
You don't have to learn it
to be able to appreciate it.

00:16:59.980 --> 00:17:02.030
So first let me try to
address this question

00:17:02.030 --> 00:17:06.260
of Feynman's cryptic comment,
the language God talks.

00:17:06.260 --> 00:17:07.093
What did he mean?

00:17:07.093 --> 00:17:09.140
I wanna try to illustrate
what I think he might have

00:17:09.140 --> 00:17:14.140
meant with an anecdote about
electricity and magnetism.

00:17:14.370 --> 00:17:17.770
So, electricity, here's a
picture of my daughter Lea,

00:17:17.770 --> 00:17:20.470
my older daughter, I have two daughters.

00:17:20.470 --> 00:17:22.760
But so when Lea was four
years old, there she is at her

00:17:22.760 --> 00:17:27.100
grandmother's house and she's
playing on the bed and it

00:17:27.100 --> 00:17:30.050
was winter and you know,
static electricity made her

00:17:30.050 --> 00:17:32.050
hair do that and I took a picture of it.

00:17:34.220 --> 00:17:36.210
I mean, you've probably
had similar experiences

00:17:36.210 --> 00:17:37.370
with static electricity.

00:17:37.370 --> 00:17:39.850
So electricity is something
we all kind of know

00:17:39.850 --> 00:17:41.910
about from playing with
static electricity,

00:17:41.910 --> 00:17:45.410
from the electricity that we
can plug into in the wall.

00:17:45.410 --> 00:17:48.440
There's also magnetism, next slide,

00:17:48.440 --> 00:17:51.540
which you know, if you take a magnet,

00:17:51.540 --> 00:17:53.660
so there's a magnet with
north and south poles.

00:17:53.660 --> 00:17:56.920
The north pole in red
and if you sprinkle these

00:17:56.920 --> 00:18:00.380
so called iron filings,
little slivers of iron

00:18:00.380 --> 00:18:04.700
on paper around the magnet
and kind of shake it so that

00:18:04.700 --> 00:18:07.250
everything has a chance
to move if it wants to,

00:18:07.250 --> 00:18:10.800
the filings form this amazing
pattern around the magnet,

00:18:10.800 --> 00:18:15.040
which we take as indication
that there's this invisible

00:18:15.040 --> 00:18:18.130
field of force around the
magnet, the magnetic field,

00:18:18.130 --> 00:18:20.490
and these filings, these
iron filings can help

00:18:20.490 --> 00:18:23.470
us visualize this
amazing pattern of force.

00:18:23.470 --> 00:18:28.320
The directions that the
magnetic field is pulling

00:18:28.320 --> 00:18:30.783
on things depending on where you are.

00:18:31.920 --> 00:18:36.880
So, you've got these concepts
of electricity and magnetism,

00:18:36.880 --> 00:18:39.890
which in the 1800's when
scientists first began to really

00:18:39.890 --> 00:18:42.890
try to understand them
scientifically by doing experiments

00:18:42.890 --> 00:18:45.300
on you know, electrical currents,

00:18:45.300 --> 00:18:47.540
magnets and the relationship between them

00:18:47.540 --> 00:18:50.940
and here I'm thinking of
people like Ampere and Faraday

00:18:50.940 --> 00:18:55.160
and Cowan, Lenz, I mean if
you've taken freshman physics

00:18:55.160 --> 00:18:56.380
you will have heard these names.

00:18:56.380 --> 00:19:00.140
So this is a great era of
discovering the laws of how

00:19:00.140 --> 00:19:03.780
magnets and electricity work
and how they're interrelated.

00:19:03.780 --> 00:19:07.150
The key concept turned out
to be the idea of a field.

00:19:07.150 --> 00:19:12.150
This invisible pattern
of force around magnets

00:19:12.450 --> 00:19:15.100
and around current
carrying wires and so on.

00:19:15.100 --> 00:19:18.170
And so Michael Faraday
had the idea of the field,

00:19:18.170 --> 00:19:21.640
but he was not very
mathematical and it was

00:19:21.640 --> 00:19:24.610
a later person named James Clerk Maxwell.

00:19:24.610 --> 00:19:25.480
Next slide.

00:19:25.480 --> 00:19:27.650
Here's a picture of
Maxwell as a young man.

00:19:27.650 --> 00:19:30.570
Usually if you know Maxwell's
pictures, he looks like an old

00:19:30.570 --> 00:19:33.490
man with a big beard and
that was him later in life,

00:19:33.490 --> 00:19:36.120
but this is Maxwell as a young man.

00:19:36.120 --> 00:19:39.510
Scottish physicist,
working in the mid 1800s

00:19:39.510 --> 00:19:42.920
and he realized that
these fields that Faraday

00:19:42.920 --> 00:19:44.580
and others were talking about could

00:19:44.580 --> 00:19:46.513
be given a mathematical description.

00:19:48.580 --> 00:19:51.340
And when he tried to give a
description of all the laws

00:19:51.340 --> 00:19:54.570
that had been discovered by
Ampere, Faraday and others,

00:19:54.570 --> 00:19:57.670
he found that calculus
was the perfect language

00:19:57.670 --> 00:19:59.930
for describing what had been discovered

00:19:59.930 --> 00:20:02.360
about electric and magnetic fields.

00:20:02.360 --> 00:20:04.260
But here's the part that gets spooky.

00:20:04.260 --> 00:20:07.440
I mean, where Feynman
uses the term the language

00:20:07.440 --> 00:20:09.060
that God talks and you
always hear people say,

00:20:09.060 --> 00:20:10.590
oh well math is a language.

00:20:10.590 --> 00:20:13.970
Yes, that's partly true,
math is a language.

00:20:13.970 --> 00:20:16.220
But it's much more than a
language and this analogy

00:20:16.220 --> 00:20:19.470
is missing something and this
is the part that I consider

00:20:19.470 --> 00:20:21.660
a bit uncanny and I
think it's what Feynman

00:20:21.660 --> 00:20:23.560
had in mind when he
said it's the language,

00:20:23.560 --> 00:20:26.800
it's not just a language,
it's the language God talks.

00:20:26.800 --> 00:20:30.390
In this sense, that when
Maxwell looked at what he had

00:20:30.390 --> 00:20:33.750
written down, his equations
that encoded all the known

00:20:33.750 --> 00:20:36.093
facts about electric and magnetic fields,

00:20:37.230 --> 00:20:39.130
he had done a translation from physics

00:20:39.130 --> 00:20:41.180
into the language of calculus,

00:20:41.180 --> 00:20:43.590
but then he could do more with
calculus, which is he could

00:20:43.590 --> 00:20:47.200
start to operate on the equations,
he could manipulate them.

00:20:47.200 --> 00:20:50.070
I mean in math we use that
word, manipulate the equations.

00:20:50.070 --> 00:20:51.930
I want you to think about it literally.

00:20:51.930 --> 00:20:53.170
Like you go to the masseuse

00:20:53.170 --> 00:20:55.940
because you have a kink in your back.

00:20:55.940 --> 00:20:58.890
She will manipulate you,
she will massage you,

00:20:58.890 --> 00:21:01.410
and she will work on you and you might

00:21:01.410 --> 00:21:03.840
start to relax and say something.

00:21:03.840 --> 00:21:06.610
And that's what happens
to Maxwell's equations.

00:21:06.610 --> 00:21:09.590
As he starts manipulating
them by adding one to another

00:21:09.590 --> 00:21:11.560
or in our language he takes the divergence

00:21:11.560 --> 00:21:13.380
of one or he takes the curl of another.

00:21:13.380 --> 00:21:15.990
He's doing mathematical
manipulations on them,

00:21:15.990 --> 00:21:17.670
trying to get them to open up and talk

00:21:17.670 --> 00:21:20.180
to him and reveal their secrets,

00:21:20.180 --> 00:21:22.210
and he doesn't know what he's looking for.

00:21:22.210 --> 00:21:25.870
He just knows that he wants
something to come out,

00:21:25.870 --> 00:21:29.610
and as he's manipulating,
at some point he sees,

00:21:29.610 --> 00:21:31.170
and by the way, what
does this mean really?

00:21:31.170 --> 00:21:32.003
Manipulating.

00:21:32.003 --> 00:21:34.470
You're transforming the
equations from one form into

00:21:34.470 --> 00:21:38.010
another form that's logically
mathematically equivalent.

00:21:38.010 --> 00:21:40.180
So in a way you're doing nothing.

00:21:40.180 --> 00:21:43.100
It's just one thing that's,
I mean if you had the logic,

00:21:43.100 --> 00:21:45.400
if you could see the chain
of reasoning in your mind,

00:21:45.400 --> 00:21:46.810
you wouldn't need the math.

00:21:46.810 --> 00:21:48.810
He's basically constructing
a long argument.

00:21:48.810 --> 00:21:51.320
That's what the symbols are doing for him.

00:21:51.320 --> 00:21:52.550
I mean that's why we use this.

00:21:52.550 --> 00:21:55.000
It's not just a language,
it's a system of reasoning.

00:21:55.000 --> 00:21:57.420
So he's doing his reasoning
on the equations and at some

00:21:57.420 --> 00:22:00.330
point he recognizes a new
equation that has come out

00:22:00.330 --> 00:22:02.280
because it's the same
equation that describes

00:22:02.280 --> 00:22:04.860
the spread of ripples on a pond.

00:22:04.860 --> 00:22:07.920
It's the equation for how waves move,

00:22:07.920 --> 00:22:11.630
and except that this is a wave
of electricity and magnetism.

00:22:11.630 --> 00:22:14.760
Where the electricity, the
electric field is generating a

00:22:14.760 --> 00:22:17.680
magnetic field, which then
regenerates the electric field,

00:22:17.680 --> 00:22:19.030
and they're doing this kind of dance,

00:22:19.030 --> 00:22:21.700
a pas de deux together dancing,

00:22:21.700 --> 00:22:23.960
propagating and he calculates.

00:22:23.960 --> 00:22:26.890
We're always interested in a
wave, how fast does it travel?

00:22:26.890 --> 00:22:29.250
He plugs in the numbers and
it turns out it propagates

00:22:29.250 --> 00:22:32.380
at the speed of light, which
had been measured around

00:22:32.380 --> 00:22:34.280
that time for the first time.

00:22:34.280 --> 00:22:35.690
So this I think, must have been one

00:22:35.690 --> 00:22:38.410
of the greatest aha
moments in human history.

00:22:38.410 --> 00:22:41.550
That in that moment, and
I wish I could be there.

00:22:41.550 --> 00:22:43.360
I mean we all wish we
could be there watching

00:22:43.360 --> 00:22:46.460
Maxwell as he realizes what light is.

00:22:46.460 --> 00:22:48.930
I mean, humanity had
known about light forever,

00:22:48.930 --> 00:22:50.970
but we never knew what it really was.

00:22:50.970 --> 00:22:52.120
Now we suddenly knew.

00:22:52.120 --> 00:22:54.810
Light is a electromagnetic wave.

00:22:54.810 --> 00:22:59.440
So, what was that that
happened there that day?

00:22:59.440 --> 00:23:02.800
I mean, that was calculus as
a language and as a system

00:23:02.800 --> 00:23:05.670
of reasoning and it was
if Maxwell had tapped into

00:23:05.670 --> 00:23:10.500
something that is built into
the structure of the universe.

00:23:10.500 --> 00:23:12.700
He wasn't just talking.

00:23:12.700 --> 00:23:14.180
I mean, he wasn't like being a poet.

00:23:14.180 --> 00:23:15.730
He was actually learning the language

00:23:15.730 --> 00:23:17.540
that the universe is speaking.

00:23:17.540 --> 00:23:21.450
So there's this prediction that
electromagnetic waves exist,

00:23:21.450 --> 00:23:23.010
that they propagate at the speed light.

00:23:23.010 --> 00:23:24.183
Next slide.

00:23:26.100 --> 00:23:27.610
Yeah, click.

00:23:27.610 --> 00:23:31.150
And so 1860s, he predicts this.

00:23:31.150 --> 00:23:33.770
Within a few years, Heinrich Hertz

00:23:33.770 --> 00:23:35.840
measures electromagnetic waves.

00:23:35.840 --> 00:23:38.140
They're real, they do exist.

00:23:38.140 --> 00:23:41.530
Soon Tesla is using them
to create the first radio

00:23:41.530 --> 00:23:44.600
systems and to do wireless
transmission of energy,

00:23:44.600 --> 00:23:47.030
and then Marconi is
sending messages across the

00:23:47.030 --> 00:23:49.700
Atlantic Ocean and you
know, you have the birth

00:23:49.700 --> 00:23:52.320
of the telegraph and
very soon after, click,

00:23:52.320 --> 00:23:57.040
we have radio, we have
television, we have wireless.

00:23:57.040 --> 00:24:00.910
So, it's not correct to say
that calculus created this,

00:24:00.910 --> 00:24:02.450
but calculus was indispensable.

00:24:02.450 --> 00:24:05.400
I mean, if it weren't for
Maxwell and this calculus,

00:24:05.400 --> 00:24:07.380
all these things that we
take for granted today,

00:24:07.380 --> 00:24:09.450
they may have still
been discovered anyway,

00:24:09.450 --> 00:24:11.780
but maybe later or maybe not at all.

00:24:11.780 --> 00:24:14.520
So, that's what I think
Feynman is talking about.

00:24:14.520 --> 00:24:17.960
That Maxwell's equations are
telling us something about

00:24:17.960 --> 00:24:20.480
the universe, not just,
it's not just a language.

00:24:20.480 --> 00:24:23.040
It's a language about something very deep.

00:24:23.040 --> 00:24:24.120
Next slide.

00:24:24.120 --> 00:24:25.770
So you may have seen these t-shirts.

00:24:25.770 --> 00:24:28.520
You know, this is a standard
nerdy t-shirt you can get,

00:24:30.451 --> 00:24:33.250
and God said, and then
there's Maxwell's equations,

00:24:33.250 --> 00:24:34.840
and then there was light.

00:24:34.840 --> 00:24:39.840
So, let's see, I don't have
a pointer but, or do I?

00:24:40.440 --> 00:24:42.410
No, I don't think I do.

00:24:42.410 --> 00:24:43.320
Do I, no.

00:24:43.320 --> 00:24:44.870
All right, I'll do it like, no.

00:24:45.870 --> 00:24:49.000
All you, you don't need to be
able to read that language,

00:24:49.000 --> 00:24:50.920
just to see that those symbols,

00:24:50.920 --> 00:24:52.810
what's going on the left where there's

00:24:52.810 --> 00:24:55.690
that upside down triangle,
that's talking about electric

00:24:55.690 --> 00:24:58.580
and magnetic fields and
how they change in space.

00:24:58.580 --> 00:25:01.180
How they change as you
move from some point.

00:25:01.180 --> 00:25:03.890
Like think of these iron
filings around the magnet.

00:25:03.890 --> 00:25:05.890
If you look at one of the
filings and then move to

00:25:05.890 --> 00:25:09.640
the next one nearby, the
direction may change a little bit.

00:25:09.640 --> 00:25:12.250
How exactly the field is changing in space

00:25:12.250 --> 00:25:14.750
is encoded by what's going on on the left.

00:25:14.750 --> 00:25:18.250
And then what's going on on
the right is you might see

00:25:18.250 --> 00:25:20.680
that there's a symbol t in the denominator

00:25:20.680 --> 00:25:22.840
of one of those fractions.

00:25:22.840 --> 00:25:24.730
That t refers to time.

00:25:24.730 --> 00:25:27.090
It's talking about how the
magnetic and electric fields

00:25:27.090 --> 00:25:30.810
are changing in time and
how that is influenced

00:25:30.810 --> 00:25:32.690
by how they're changing in space.

00:25:32.690 --> 00:25:35.200
So there's this interaction
between you know,

00:25:35.200 --> 00:25:38.140
things happening in space
and time encoded in this

00:25:38.140 --> 00:25:40.890
and like I say, these
four equations then imply

00:25:40.890 --> 00:25:42.870
the existence of electromagnetic waves,

00:25:42.870 --> 00:25:44.360
although it's not obvious,
you have to be good

00:25:44.360 --> 00:25:47.610
at calculus and this
particular part of calculus

00:25:47.610 --> 00:25:50.250
called differential equations,
which is what these are.

00:25:50.250 --> 00:25:52.250
These are four differential equations.

00:25:52.250 --> 00:25:55.440
The word differential meaning
that they express how things

00:25:55.440 --> 00:25:56.960
change from one point to another

00:25:56.960 --> 00:25:58.760
or from one moment to another.

00:25:58.760 --> 00:26:00.950
Those are differences in time or space.

00:26:00.950 --> 00:26:03.000
So that's the difference in differential.

00:26:04.270 --> 00:26:05.600
Okay, let's move on.

00:26:05.600 --> 00:26:07.370
So, next slide.

00:26:07.370 --> 00:26:10.500
Now, so the larger point
though, I mean Maxwell is just

00:26:10.500 --> 00:26:12.130
supposed to illustrate this broader point.

00:26:12.130 --> 00:26:14.120
Why calculus matters, this is my claim.

00:26:14.120 --> 00:26:16.000
Sort of the thesis of the whole book.

00:26:16.000 --> 00:26:17.550
So if you wanna just get the punch line,

00:26:17.550 --> 00:26:19.580
they say it's good to give the
punch line at the beginning

00:26:19.580 --> 00:26:21.590
'cause people will
space out and get tired.

00:26:21.590 --> 00:26:23.400
So I'm giving it to you right now.

00:26:23.400 --> 00:26:25.450
The rest you can start checking your email

00:26:25.450 --> 00:26:26.830
or whatever you need to do.

00:26:26.830 --> 00:26:28.270
But why does calculus matter?

00:26:28.270 --> 00:26:29.350
Here's the argument.

00:26:29.350 --> 00:26:32.860
First, because the laws of
nature happen to be written

00:26:32.860 --> 00:26:36.020
in this particular language,
in the language of calculus.

00:26:36.020 --> 00:26:38.700
Second, because calculus
is more than a language,

00:26:38.700 --> 00:26:41.910
it's a system of reasoning
that taps into something

00:26:41.910 --> 00:26:43.830
about the structure of the universe.

00:26:43.830 --> 00:26:45.960
It's as if the universe runs on calculus,

00:26:45.960 --> 00:26:47.810
if you want me to say it that way.

00:26:47.810 --> 00:26:49.820
I mean, I'm getting a
little mystical here,

00:26:49.820 --> 00:26:53.620
but I think it's true in that
we see this again and again.

00:26:53.620 --> 00:26:55.970
Not just with the laws of
electricity and magnetism,

00:26:55.970 --> 00:26:58.790
but if you look at Einstein's
laws about how gravity works

00:26:58.790 --> 00:27:00.610
encoded in general relatively,

00:27:00.610 --> 00:27:02.760
which I was just listening
to in Priya's book,

00:27:02.760 --> 00:27:05.430
Mapping the Heavens, which I
highly, highly recommend if you

00:27:05.430 --> 00:27:08.730
want a great book about
astronomy and astrophysics.

00:27:08.730 --> 00:27:11.630
But, so Einstein's equations
are differential equations.

00:27:11.630 --> 00:27:15.030
Newton's laws of motion and
gravity, differential equations.

00:27:15.030 --> 00:27:16.750
The laws of how fluids move like

00:27:16.750 --> 00:27:19.280
air and water, differential equations.

00:27:19.280 --> 00:27:21.550
Everything under the
sun that we understand

00:27:21.550 --> 00:27:24.650
in mathematical terms,
we understand through

00:27:24.650 --> 00:27:27.390
differential equations, i.e. calculus.

00:27:27.390 --> 00:27:29.233
It is the language God talks.

00:27:30.150 --> 00:27:30.983
Next.

00:27:32.240 --> 00:27:34.550
By learning to speak
this strange language,

00:27:34.550 --> 00:27:36.870
I mean it took humanity
thousands of years to learn this

00:27:36.870 --> 00:27:40.480
language, but by learning
to speak it and discovering

00:27:40.480 --> 00:27:43.490
that it is somehow tapping
into this deep structure

00:27:43.490 --> 00:27:44.843
of the laws of nature.

00:27:46.590 --> 00:27:48.540
We've been able to use calculus, you know,

00:27:48.540 --> 00:27:50.610
in combination with
science and technology,

00:27:50.610 --> 00:27:51.900
to remake the world.

00:27:51.900 --> 00:27:52.900
That's the argument.

00:27:53.810 --> 00:27:55.350
Now, how did this all happen?

00:27:55.350 --> 00:27:56.810
I mean we didn't start with Maxwell.

00:27:56.810 --> 00:27:59.030
Remember, Maxwell was at
the time of the Civil War,

00:27:59.030 --> 00:28:01.133
that's 1860 something.

00:28:02.260 --> 00:28:03.620
We didn't begin with that.

00:28:03.620 --> 00:28:06.660
We began thousands of years earlier.

00:28:06.660 --> 00:28:09.880
It's commonly, you know, many
books will tell you calculus

00:28:09.880 --> 00:28:13.050
was invented by Newton
and Leibniz in the 1600s.

00:28:13.050 --> 00:28:14.770
I don't think that's
the right point of view.

00:28:14.770 --> 00:28:16.410
I think that's way too parochial

00:28:16.410 --> 00:28:18.999
a view of what was really happening.

00:28:18.999 --> 00:28:21.990
I would much rather
say we started calculus

00:28:21.990 --> 00:28:23.693
a few thousand years before that.

00:28:24.930 --> 00:28:25.910
Next slide.

00:28:25.910 --> 00:28:27.420
So I'll try to explain to why.

00:28:27.420 --> 00:28:30.780
And to do this let me try
to personify calculus.

00:28:30.780 --> 00:28:32.370
I think it's the easiest
way to think about it.

00:28:32.370 --> 00:28:34.120
Think of it as if it were a person.

00:28:38.780 --> 00:28:42.490
That is, not really a person
of course, it's a subject,

00:28:42.490 --> 00:28:45.440
but when I say what calculus
wants, I guess you could then

00:28:45.440 --> 00:28:47.800
if you're very literally
minded you should hear that as,

00:28:47.800 --> 00:28:49.873
what the practitioners of calculus want.

00:28:50.790 --> 00:28:53.390
The people who do calculus,
what is it that we want?

00:28:54.650 --> 00:28:56.710
The books, textbooks about calculus

00:28:56.710 --> 00:28:58.950
are like 1,000 pages
long, they're very heavy,

00:28:58.950 --> 00:29:01.514
they weigh like cinder block weight.

00:29:01.514 --> 00:29:02.450
What is in there?

00:29:02.450 --> 00:29:03.450
What are we trying to do?

00:29:03.450 --> 00:29:06.510
It looks very complicated,
but that's misleading.

00:29:06.510 --> 00:29:09.913
What calculus wants is simplicity.

00:29:11.210 --> 00:29:14.010
The reason it looks so bulky
is it's tackling very hard,

00:29:14.010 --> 00:29:16.940
complicated problems, and it
wants to make them simpler.

00:29:16.940 --> 00:29:19.670
Calculus is all about what
do you do to make difficult

00:29:19.670 --> 00:29:23.100
problems easier and it
has a grand strategy,

00:29:23.100 --> 00:29:25.210
and this strategy is buried under all

00:29:25.210 --> 00:29:27.720
the minutiae of derivatives and integrals

00:29:27.720 --> 00:29:29.570
for those of you who have taken calculus.

00:29:29.570 --> 00:29:31.190
You learned about trig substitutions

00:29:31.190 --> 00:29:32.910
and every other possible trick.

00:29:32.910 --> 00:29:34.620
That stuff is distracting.

00:29:34.620 --> 00:29:35.730
Here's the real point.

00:29:35.730 --> 00:29:37.160
What is calculus really about?

00:29:37.160 --> 00:29:38.780
I will now tell you.

00:29:38.780 --> 00:29:40.440
Next.

00:29:40.440 --> 00:29:41.860
Here's how it gets what it wants.

00:29:41.860 --> 00:29:44.660
It has one big idea and
this idea runs like a theme,

00:29:44.660 --> 00:29:46.920
you know in a musical,
theme and variations.

00:29:46.920 --> 00:29:50.450
There is one idea in calculus
and if you get this one idea,

00:29:50.450 --> 00:29:52.290
which for some reason we
never tell the students

00:29:52.290 --> 00:29:55.703
this idea, but everybody
who knows calculus knows it.

00:29:56.820 --> 00:29:58.780
Jeff the mathematician will tell you,

00:29:58.780 --> 00:30:00.590
I'll check this on you,
you tell me if you agree.

00:30:00.590 --> 00:30:01.913
This is the one big idea.

00:30:02.900 --> 00:30:05.570
The big idea is that we're
gonna make problems easier

00:30:05.570 --> 00:30:07.960
by slicing them into smaller problems.

00:30:07.960 --> 00:30:09.450
Now that is an idea everybody

00:30:09.450 --> 00:30:11.250
has who knows how to solve problems.

00:30:11.250 --> 00:30:13.540
To make a hard problem
easier, make it smaller.

00:30:13.540 --> 00:30:15.840
Break it into parts and work on the parts.

00:30:15.840 --> 00:30:17.240
That's an ancient idea.

00:30:17.240 --> 00:30:20.660
The crazy idea of calculus is don't stop.

00:30:20.660 --> 00:30:22.210
Do that forever.

00:30:22.210 --> 00:30:23.850
Do that at infinitum.

00:30:23.850 --> 00:30:24.740
Next.

00:30:24.740 --> 00:30:25.620
This is the big idea.

00:30:25.620 --> 00:30:27.720
I'm gonna call it the infinity principle.

00:30:27.720 --> 00:30:30.170
Nobody else calls it that,
I'm just making it up.

00:30:31.070 --> 00:30:33.200
But this is what I think
is the heart of calculus.

00:30:33.200 --> 00:30:36.240
Is that you can make problems
easier by slicing 'em and

00:30:36.240 --> 00:30:39.780
slicing 'em and slicing 'em
all the way to infinity, next.

00:30:39.780 --> 00:30:41.310
So you keep doing that forever

00:30:41.310 --> 00:30:42.980
and then what are you left with?

00:30:42.980 --> 00:30:44.410
You're left, next.

00:30:44.410 --> 00:30:46.170
You're left with infinitely many,

00:30:46.170 --> 00:30:48.220
infinitesimally tiny pieces

00:30:48.220 --> 00:30:51.580
and the philosophy is that those pieces,

00:30:51.580 --> 00:30:55.040
whether they're short pieces
of a curve or little patches of

00:30:55.040 --> 00:30:59.910
a surface or an instance in
time or tiny bits of material,

00:30:59.910 --> 00:31:02.580
whatever it is, that tiny
thing is gonna be much

00:31:02.580 --> 00:31:05.050
more manageable than
the original big thing.

00:31:05.050 --> 00:31:08.390
And so the strategy is
a two staged strategy.

00:31:08.390 --> 00:31:09.840
First you cut the thing down

00:31:09.840 --> 00:31:13.150
to the smallest infinitesimal bits, next.

00:31:13.150 --> 00:31:15.060
You solve the problem for the tiny pieces.

00:31:15.060 --> 00:31:17.120
That turns out usually to be fairly easy.

00:31:17.120 --> 00:31:18.660
That's why you did this.

00:31:18.660 --> 00:31:19.493
Next.

00:31:19.493 --> 00:31:20.960
Then you have to put the answers back

00:31:20.960 --> 00:31:22.760
together to get the original whole.

00:31:22.760 --> 00:31:25.130
That tends to be hard, very hard.

00:31:25.130 --> 00:31:26.490
There's no free lunch.

00:31:26.490 --> 00:31:29.330
So putting the pieces back together

00:31:29.330 --> 00:31:30.760
is what makes calculus hard,

00:31:30.760 --> 00:31:32.890
but it turns out it will
be easier than dealing

00:31:32.890 --> 00:31:35.670
with the original problem
without this strategy.

00:31:35.670 --> 00:31:37.290
So this is the best thing
we've ever thought of.

00:31:37.290 --> 00:31:39.980
It's one of the great
ideas in human history.

00:31:39.980 --> 00:31:43.713
Up there with human rights,
democracy, evolution,

00:31:44.930 --> 00:31:45.950
quantum mechanics.

00:31:45.950 --> 00:31:48.590
I mean, this one idea,
the infinity principle

00:31:48.590 --> 00:31:50.770
has had as big of a consequential impact

00:31:50.770 --> 00:31:53.840
on the world as I think,
just about any other idea.

00:31:53.840 --> 00:31:55.210
Next.

00:31:55.210 --> 00:31:56.570
So, the jargon.

00:31:56.570 --> 00:31:58.240
You've heard of differential calculus.

00:31:58.240 --> 00:32:00.590
That's what we call the
operation of slicing

00:32:00.590 --> 00:32:03.120
and taking the tiny pieces.

00:32:03.120 --> 00:32:05.900
Calculating derivatives amounts to this.

00:32:05.900 --> 00:32:07.780
Putting them back together,
that's the hard part.

00:32:07.780 --> 00:32:09.017
That's called integral calculus.

00:32:09.017 --> 00:32:11.000
And so if you've taken
calculus you know that

00:32:11.000 --> 00:32:13.750
you learn derivatives before
integrals, that's why.

00:32:13.750 --> 00:32:17.790
Because differential calculus
is easier, you do that first.

00:32:17.790 --> 00:32:21.023
Now, there's a key assumption
in this when we say

00:32:21.023 --> 00:32:24.120
that we're gonna chop problems
into their tiniest bits,

00:32:24.120 --> 00:32:26.920
we're assuming that we
can do that forever.

00:32:26.920 --> 00:32:29.620
This is the importance
of being continuous.

00:32:29.620 --> 00:32:31.230
Next.

00:32:31.230 --> 00:32:33.490
The infinity principle
says that it will work.

00:32:33.490 --> 00:32:35.970
I mean, this strategy will
work only on those objects

00:32:35.970 --> 00:32:39.860
that can be infinitely
subdivided, endlessly, okay?

00:32:39.860 --> 00:32:43.117
If that's not gonna work, oh,
then you can't use calculus.

00:32:43.117 --> 00:32:45.270
So the calculus only works on these things

00:32:45.270 --> 00:32:47.170
that infinitely subdivisible.

00:32:47.170 --> 00:32:49.140
We call those things continuous.

00:32:49.140 --> 00:32:52.350
Meaning from the old Latin roots,

00:32:52.350 --> 00:32:55.543
what con plus tinerae would
mean holding together.

00:32:56.590 --> 00:32:58.060
So a continuous thing is that which

00:32:58.060 --> 00:32:59.710
holds together in that sense.

00:32:59.710 --> 00:33:02.833
That it's all touching itself.

00:33:03.910 --> 00:33:06.973
So continuous objects are
grist for calculus, next.

00:33:07.900 --> 00:33:09.630
But notice the creative fantasy.

00:33:09.630 --> 00:33:12.540
In case you think math is all
very objective and rigorous,

00:33:12.540 --> 00:33:14.880
I want you to, don't wanna
disabuse you of that idea.

00:33:14.880 --> 00:33:16.400
That's only half of math.

00:33:16.400 --> 00:33:17.570
That's the second half.

00:33:17.570 --> 00:33:19.920
The first half is creative fantasy.

00:33:19.920 --> 00:33:22.030
You have to have
imagination and creativity.

00:33:22.030 --> 00:33:23.440
All math is like that.

00:33:23.440 --> 00:33:25.330
Then you tidy up and make things nice

00:33:25.330 --> 00:33:27.690
and logically pristine,
but at the beginning

00:33:27.690 --> 00:33:31.040
it's wild fantasy, imagination and desire.

00:33:31.040 --> 00:33:32.960
And in this case, the desire is to pretend

00:33:32.960 --> 00:33:34.450
that the world is like this.

00:33:34.450 --> 00:33:36.490
Pretend that everything can be infinitely

00:33:36.490 --> 00:33:38.160
divided as much as you want.

00:33:38.160 --> 00:33:39.570
Why is it fantasy?

00:33:39.570 --> 00:33:41.320
Because that's not really correct.

00:33:41.320 --> 00:33:42.660
The world is not like that.

00:33:42.660 --> 00:33:45.640
We know today, depending who you talk to,

00:33:45.640 --> 00:33:48.120
but you know, this was a debate going back

00:33:48.120 --> 00:33:49.900
to the, you know, the ancients.

00:33:49.900 --> 00:33:53.180
When you think about atoms,
what's the word atom mean?

00:33:53.180 --> 00:33:56.210
It means literally atom,
it means uncuttable, right?

00:33:56.210 --> 00:33:58.040
The things, things that cannot be cut.

00:33:58.040 --> 00:34:00.800
The smallest bit of matter is an atom

00:34:00.800 --> 00:34:02.990
that cannot be cut any further.

00:34:02.990 --> 00:34:04.250
And so the Greeks, you know,

00:34:04.250 --> 00:34:07.170
in the time of Democritus,
were arguing do atoms exist?

00:34:07.170 --> 00:34:09.790
Is the world grainy, is
it made of tiny things?

00:34:09.790 --> 00:34:12.040
Or is it infinitely subdivisible forever?

00:34:12.040 --> 00:34:14.180
And not just matter, but space and time.

00:34:14.180 --> 00:34:16.940
Is there a smallest interval of time?

00:34:16.940 --> 00:34:19.960
Is there a smallest amount of space?

00:34:19.960 --> 00:34:21.860
This is a live question today.

00:34:21.860 --> 00:34:23.340
If you talk to the string theorists

00:34:23.340 --> 00:34:26.230
or people doing quantum
gravity, they will tell you yes,

00:34:26.230 --> 00:34:27.690
there is a smallest thing.

00:34:27.690 --> 00:34:30.550
It's called the Planck
scale, after Max Planck.

00:34:30.550 --> 00:34:32.230
There's the smallest unit of space.

00:34:32.230 --> 00:34:34.960
Something like 10 to the minus 35 meters.

00:34:34.960 --> 00:34:36.067
Way, way, way smaller than

00:34:36.067 --> 00:34:38.393
the smallest particle that we know.

00:34:39.880 --> 00:34:42.040
There's a smallest unit
of time, which is the time

00:34:42.040 --> 00:34:44.660
it would take light to
travel that distance

00:34:44.660 --> 00:34:46.880
and there's nothing
smaller that we know of.

00:34:46.880 --> 00:34:49.910
So it is not true according
to modern physics,

00:34:49.910 --> 00:34:52.910
that you can infinitely subdivide
time and space and matter.

00:34:53.750 --> 00:34:55.760
Do you think we care
about that in calculus?

00:34:55.760 --> 00:34:56.690
Yes and no.

00:34:56.690 --> 00:34:59.490
Okay, we wanna get things
right, but we also want to make

00:34:59.490 --> 00:35:01.600
progress and so we're gonna pretend.

00:35:01.600 --> 00:35:03.190
That's what I mean by creative fantasy.

00:35:03.190 --> 00:35:05.940
We will pretend that the world
is infinitely subdivisible.

00:35:05.940 --> 00:35:07.560
Next.

00:35:07.560 --> 00:35:10.040
So calculus then in my
definition and essentially

00:35:10.040 --> 00:35:13.180
nobody else's, but I think
it's really the heart of it,

00:35:13.180 --> 00:35:15.990
is calculus should be thought of as a use

00:35:15.990 --> 00:35:18.530
of the infinity principle to solve

00:35:18.530 --> 00:35:20.620
or to shed light on
anything that's continuous.

00:35:20.620 --> 00:35:24.610
Whether it's a shape, an
object, something moving,

00:35:24.610 --> 00:35:27.373
any kind of phenomenon,
that's the strategy.

00:35:28.270 --> 00:35:31.100
And so, if I had to boil
it down to what calculus

00:35:31.100 --> 00:35:34.020
has really been obsessed
with, it's three things.

00:35:34.020 --> 00:35:35.470
Next.

00:35:35.470 --> 00:35:37.210
Oh, okay.

00:35:37.210 --> 00:35:41.180
The three are, curves and curved shapes.

00:35:41.180 --> 00:35:44.300
So geometry, but specially about
things not made of straight

00:35:44.300 --> 00:35:48.170
lines and flat planes, but
that are curved, in sinuous.

00:35:48.170 --> 00:35:50.190
That was a big problem
in ancient geometry.

00:35:50.190 --> 00:35:51.650
Calculus came into solve that

00:35:51.650 --> 00:35:54.750
and that's where it became,
dealing with curves.

00:35:54.750 --> 00:35:56.820
So that, let me go back, sorry.

00:35:56.820 --> 00:35:57.840
Let's just go back one.

00:35:57.840 --> 00:36:01.750
So yeah, so we're gonna start with curves,

00:36:01.750 --> 00:36:04.250
but calculus has, you know,
the three things that it

00:36:04.250 --> 00:36:07.980
has focused on throughout
its like 2,500 year history,

00:36:07.980 --> 00:36:10.000
curves, motion and change.

00:36:10.000 --> 00:36:12.030
So if you want a little
mantra, that's your manta.

00:36:12.030 --> 00:36:14.190
Curves, motion and change.

00:36:14.190 --> 00:36:15.970
Some people like to define calculus

00:36:15.970 --> 00:36:18.100
as the mathematics of change.

00:36:18.100 --> 00:36:19.650
That works pretty well actually,

00:36:19.650 --> 00:36:23.140
because a curve you can think
of as changing direction,

00:36:23.140 --> 00:36:26.730
and motion you could think
of as changing position.

00:36:26.730 --> 00:36:29.270
So if you had to say it in one word,

00:36:29.270 --> 00:36:31.770
calculus is the mathematics of change.

00:36:31.770 --> 00:36:34.340
But specifically, continuous change.

00:36:34.340 --> 00:36:36.920
Never ending, ongoing change.

00:36:36.920 --> 00:36:38.410
Okay, next.

00:36:38.410 --> 00:36:39.450
So let's begin with curves.

00:36:39.450 --> 00:36:41.010
Now here's an ancient problem.

00:36:41.010 --> 00:36:43.510
Figuring out properties of a circle.

00:36:43.510 --> 00:36:44.730
You might think that's trivial,

00:36:44.730 --> 00:36:46.740
I learned that in high school geometry.

00:36:46.740 --> 00:36:49.980
It's not trivial, it's
not easy to figure out.

00:36:49.980 --> 00:36:52.400
Like think of how weird
the number pi is, right?

00:36:52.400 --> 00:36:54.720
Everybody is fascinated
by pi with its infinitely

00:36:54.720 --> 00:36:57.250
many digits that don't show any pattern.

00:36:57.250 --> 00:36:58.720
Why is pi so weird?

00:36:58.720 --> 00:37:01.090
Because pi is a creature of calculus,

00:37:01.090 --> 00:37:03.320
it's not a create of geometry.

00:37:03.320 --> 00:37:06.070
The fact that it has these
infinitely many digits that don't

00:37:06.070 --> 00:37:08.160
repeat and don't show a
pattern is already a clue that

00:37:08.160 --> 00:37:11.110
there's something with the
infinity principle at work here.

00:37:12.302 --> 00:37:14.350
You know, in high school
you just memorize pi.

00:37:14.350 --> 00:37:16.280
Maybe you didn't think
why is that the number?

00:37:16.280 --> 00:37:18.240
How does anyone calculate that number?

00:37:18.240 --> 00:37:20.000
You have to use calculus, that's how.

00:37:20.000 --> 00:37:22.550
So let me remind you, pi
is defined as the ratio

00:37:22.550 --> 00:37:25.143
of the circumference of
the circle to its diameter.

00:37:26.010 --> 00:37:27.510
Okay, circumference the distance around,

00:37:27.510 --> 00:37:29.520
diameter the distance across.

00:37:29.520 --> 00:37:34.220
But a big question, if
that's the definition of pi,

00:37:34.220 --> 00:37:36.520
fine, that's one property of the circle.

00:37:36.520 --> 00:37:38.560
What about the space inside the circle?

00:37:38.560 --> 00:37:40.020
The area inside the circle?

00:37:40.020 --> 00:37:42.350
You may have memorized
a formula for the SATs,

00:37:42.350 --> 00:37:44.310
pi r squared, the area of a circle.

00:37:44.310 --> 00:37:46.950
R is the radius, that's half the diameter.

00:37:46.950 --> 00:37:48.020
The distance from the center

00:37:48.020 --> 00:37:50.143
of the circle out to the edge there.

00:37:51.000 --> 00:37:53.250
Where does that formula
pi r squared come from?

00:37:53.250 --> 00:37:55.320
That's a calculus result.

00:37:55.320 --> 00:37:57.590
You will not find it in Euclid by the way.

00:37:57.590 --> 00:37:59.470
I mean if you think
it's Euclidean geometry,

00:37:59.470 --> 00:38:00.540
you don't understand.

00:38:00.540 --> 00:38:01.410
You can look at Euclid,

00:38:01.410 --> 00:38:03.457
you will not find pi r squared in Euclid.

00:38:03.457 --> 00:38:06.450
Euclid does say the area
of a circle is proportional

00:38:06.450 --> 00:38:09.623
to the square of the radius,
but he doesn't have pi.

00:38:11.270 --> 00:38:13.200
For him pi is not even a number.

00:38:13.200 --> 00:38:16.130
It's you know, he okay,
so you have to wait

00:38:16.130 --> 00:38:18.880
till like two more
generations for Archimedes

00:38:18.880 --> 00:38:21.980
and that's when we start
to really begin calculus.

00:38:21.980 --> 00:38:23.050
He's the great maestro

00:38:23.050 --> 00:38:24.700
of the infinity principle, Archimedes.

00:38:24.700 --> 00:38:26.420
So let's see the next slide.

00:38:26.420 --> 00:38:27.253
Here's an argument.

00:38:27.253 --> 00:38:29.390
This is not really Archimedes' argument,

00:38:29.390 --> 00:38:31.370
it's close in spirit to his argument

00:38:31.370 --> 00:38:33.140
for calculating the area of a circle.

00:38:33.140 --> 00:38:37.100
But if you've never seen
it, I wanna show it to you

00:38:37.100 --> 00:38:39.260
'cause I think it's very
dramatic and it's an example

00:38:39.260 --> 00:38:41.903
of what it feels like to
have an aha moment in math.

00:38:43.400 --> 00:38:46.580
Okay, so what I'm doing
there is I'm taking a circle

00:38:46.580 --> 00:38:48.300
and I've chopped it into four pieces.

00:38:48.300 --> 00:38:50.443
You could think of them as
slices of pizza if you want.

00:38:50.443 --> 00:38:54.230
It's an abstract mathematical
pizza and I'm rearranging

00:38:54.230 --> 00:38:57.470
those slices into this
funny shape on the bottom

00:38:57.470 --> 00:39:00.990
which I'll refer to as a
scalloped shape because you know,

00:39:00.990 --> 00:39:05.540
it's got these bulbus
curves on the bottom.

00:39:05.540 --> 00:39:07.900
And I've put on the bottom
pi r to indicate that

00:39:07.900 --> 00:39:10.550
the amount of crust, if you
thought of it as a pizza,

00:39:11.480 --> 00:39:13.020
the length of the crust on the bottom

00:39:13.020 --> 00:39:15.700
is pi times the radius,
why am I saying that?

00:39:15.700 --> 00:39:18.220
The whole crust is two pi
times the radius, right?

00:39:18.220 --> 00:39:19.730
That's the circumference.

00:39:19.730 --> 00:39:23.000
Pi times the diameter, the
diameter is two times the radius.

00:39:23.000 --> 00:39:25.560
So two pi r is the circumference.

00:39:25.560 --> 00:39:27.710
Half of the circumference,
half of the curvy part

00:39:27.710 --> 00:39:30.210
is on the bottom, half is on the top.

00:39:30.210 --> 00:39:33.220
So it's pi r on the bottom and
then the edge of the pizza,

00:39:33.220 --> 00:39:36.160
that straight piece, this piece,

00:39:36.160 --> 00:39:38.580
that was, that's just
the radius of the pizza.

00:39:38.580 --> 00:39:40.720
So at the moment, the strategy is,

00:39:40.720 --> 00:39:42.920
if we could figure out
the area of that thing,

00:39:42.920 --> 00:39:45.090
we would then know the area of the circle.

00:39:45.090 --> 00:39:47.630
The trouble is we've looked
like we made the problem worse.

00:39:47.630 --> 00:39:50.030
That shape is harder than the circle.

00:39:50.030 --> 00:39:52.920
But what we're gonna try to do is somehow

00:39:52.920 --> 00:39:54.710
with the use of the infinity principle,

00:39:54.710 --> 00:39:58.230
change the circle into a
shape whose area we know.

00:39:58.230 --> 00:40:00.150
That's the grand strategy.

00:40:00.150 --> 00:40:02.060
All right, so next slide.

00:40:02.060 --> 00:40:04.410
The thought is, maybe
the reason that shape

00:40:04.410 --> 00:40:07.460
on the bottom was bad is we
didn't take enough slices.

00:40:07.460 --> 00:40:09.010
So instead of taking four slices,

00:40:09.010 --> 00:40:12.230
what if you take eight and
you arrange them like this?

00:40:12.230 --> 00:40:15.000
Can you see that the
shape is getting better?

00:40:15.000 --> 00:40:17.900
It looks like it's trying to turn

00:40:17.900 --> 00:40:19.330
into some shape you recognize.

00:40:19.330 --> 00:40:20.770
Anyone wanna volunteer what shape

00:40:20.770 --> 00:40:22.563
it looks like it's trying to become?

00:40:22.563 --> 00:40:23.396
- [Student] Parallelogram.

00:40:23.396 --> 00:40:24.264
- A what?

00:40:24.264 --> 00:40:25.150
- Parallelogram.
- It looks like it's

00:40:25.150 --> 00:40:26.780
trying to be a parallelogram, right?

00:40:26.780 --> 00:40:28.270
It's almost a parallelogram 'cause

00:40:28.270 --> 00:40:30.890
this side is parallel to that side.

00:40:30.890 --> 00:40:32.910
The bottom is not straight
like it's supposed

00:40:32.910 --> 00:40:35.160
to be in a parallelogram,
a little bit curvy.

00:40:35.160 --> 00:40:39.190
But here I wanna test your
dexterity for a second.

00:40:39.190 --> 00:40:41.040
Jeff, can you toggle back and forth.

00:40:41.040 --> 00:40:43.740
Well what I mean is when
you go to the optometrist,

00:40:43.740 --> 00:40:47.210
they will sometimes say
better, worse, better, worse.

00:40:47.210 --> 00:40:48.590
Can you do that between the two slides?

00:40:48.590 --> 00:40:49.910
Better, worse.

00:40:49.910 --> 00:40:51.180
Which is better?

00:40:51.180 --> 00:40:52.013
Better.

00:40:52.013 --> 00:40:54.160
Okay, go one more forward.

00:40:54.160 --> 00:40:55.310
Better.

00:40:55.310 --> 00:40:56.963
Okay, that's with 16.

00:40:57.870 --> 00:40:58.750
That's better.

00:40:58.750 --> 00:41:00.470
Now can you see what's happening.

00:41:00.470 --> 00:41:03.480
Actually, keep your eye
on the tilt of this side.

00:41:03.480 --> 00:41:04.313
Can we do that?

00:41:04.313 --> 00:41:05.150
Go back one.

00:41:05.150 --> 00:41:06.850
See it's more titled and more.

00:41:06.850 --> 00:41:09.220
So now going forward
it's getting less tilted.

00:41:09.220 --> 00:41:12.270
If you did this infinitely
often, you'd have infinitely

00:41:12.270 --> 00:41:15.320
many infinitesimally thin slices,

00:41:15.320 --> 00:41:19.383
but they would be standing
upright and you would get that.

00:41:20.400 --> 00:41:21.700
Isn't that cool?

00:41:21.700 --> 00:41:24.980
And notice the whole time
the bottom was always length,

00:41:24.980 --> 00:41:27.910
half the circle pi r, and
this was always the radius.

00:41:27.910 --> 00:41:30.640
So now you know how to find
the area of a rectangle.

00:41:30.640 --> 00:41:33.330
The length times the height.

00:41:33.330 --> 00:41:34.880
Uh yeah, pi r times r.

00:41:34.880 --> 00:41:35.880
That's pi r squared.

00:41:35.880 --> 00:41:38.620
That is basically the real
reason the area of a circle

00:41:38.620 --> 00:41:40.670
is pi r squared, because
it can be morphed into

00:41:40.670 --> 00:41:44.200
a rectangle with the help
of the infinity principle.

00:41:44.200 --> 00:41:46.980
Now, if you're really a
tight mathematician out there

00:41:46.980 --> 00:41:49.100
you know I've done some fudging here.

00:41:49.100 --> 00:41:50.330
Just relax.

00:41:50.330 --> 00:41:51.910
(audience laughing)

00:41:51.910 --> 00:41:55.070
This is basically correct, but you know,

00:41:55.070 --> 00:41:57.290
there's some loose
moments in the argument.

00:41:57.290 --> 00:41:58.770
But remember what the venue is.

00:41:58.770 --> 00:42:00.373
Come on, give me a break, okay.

00:42:00.373 --> 00:42:02.350
(chuckling)

00:42:02.350 --> 00:42:03.650
But that's the gist of it.

00:42:03.650 --> 00:42:06.640
Okay, so that's an example
of the power of infinity.

00:42:06.640 --> 00:42:08.120
That the, also notice that

00:42:08.120 --> 00:42:10.430
the shape becomes best at infinity.

00:42:10.430 --> 00:42:12.010
You know the optometrist test.

00:42:12.010 --> 00:42:14.420
Keep going, things get better at infinity.

00:42:14.420 --> 00:42:17.240
That's a key principle in calculus.

00:42:17.240 --> 00:42:19.350
Things are better at infinity.

00:42:19.350 --> 00:42:20.183
Okay, next.

00:42:21.140 --> 00:42:23.260
So as I said earlier, there
are these three obsessions:

00:42:23.260 --> 00:42:24.567
curves, motion and change.

00:42:24.567 --> 00:42:27.250
And for the sake of
accessibility I'm probably gonna

00:42:27.250 --> 00:42:30.470
mostly talk about curves,
it's the least technical.

00:42:30.470 --> 00:42:33.360
But we can talk about how
calculus has revolutionized

00:42:33.360 --> 00:42:35.670
our understanding on things
that move on the Earth

00:42:35.670 --> 00:42:38.770
and in the heavens and
anything that changes.

00:42:38.770 --> 00:42:40.870
Whether it's traffic on the highway,

00:42:40.870 --> 00:42:45.860
whether it's the level of
HIV virus in the bloodstream

00:42:45.860 --> 00:42:47.490
of patients who are infected,

00:42:47.490 --> 00:42:50.193
I mean it's been applied to
everything under the sun.

00:42:51.240 --> 00:42:52.560
Next.

00:42:52.560 --> 00:42:55.090
So back to curves just
because I wanna show you

00:42:55.090 --> 00:42:57.173
one more masterpiece of Archimedes.

00:42:58.600 --> 00:43:00.530
To show you another little
bit of how the infinity,

00:43:00.530 --> 00:43:03.580
this is actually more
honestly what Archimedes did.

00:43:03.580 --> 00:43:06.260
Take a circle, you can
make shapes with a circle.

00:43:06.260 --> 00:43:07.490
I mean they started with circles.

00:43:07.490 --> 00:43:10.150
That was the fundamentally
first mysterious shape,

00:43:10.150 --> 00:43:12.300
and then you could make
shapes like a cone,

00:43:12.300 --> 00:43:16.350
and if you slice a cone with
planes of different tilts,

00:43:16.350 --> 00:43:17.910
you can make a shape like that called

00:43:17.910 --> 00:43:20.880
an ellipse or you can
make a shape like this.

00:43:20.880 --> 00:43:22.610
If you slice parallel to the side

00:43:22.610 --> 00:43:24.970
of the cone you make a
shape called a parabola.

00:43:24.970 --> 00:43:27.030
So originally these were
thought of as sections

00:43:27.030 --> 00:43:30.170
of a cone and they're still
called conic sections.

00:43:30.170 --> 00:43:31.927
Ellipses and parabolas
and also hyperbolas,

00:43:31.927 --> 00:43:34.560
but let's not bother with them for now.

00:43:34.560 --> 00:43:36.240
So next.

00:43:36.240 --> 00:43:38.900
So one question that
Archimedes wondered about,

00:43:38.900 --> 00:43:42.970
this is like 250 B.C., he's
in Syracuse on the island

00:43:42.970 --> 00:43:45.760
of Sicily, part of the
larger Greek empire.

00:43:45.760 --> 00:43:47.440
The Roman's would like to invade into,

00:43:47.440 --> 00:43:50.700
in fact they do invade
and try to take over.

00:43:50.700 --> 00:43:53.800
Archimedes you know, takes
some time away from his math

00:43:53.800 --> 00:43:57.710
to build war machines that
according to legends, you know,

00:43:57.710 --> 00:44:00.360
Plutarch tells us in his
history that Archimedes

00:44:00.360 --> 00:44:02.150
made these fearsome
machines that could grab

00:44:02.150 --> 00:44:03.820
the Roman ships out of the ocean.

00:44:03.820 --> 00:44:07.260
Giant cranes lift them up, shake them so

00:44:07.260 --> 00:44:08.960
that the soldiers would, you know,

00:44:08.960 --> 00:44:10.810
the sailors would come out
like if you were shaking

00:44:10.810 --> 00:44:13.560
sand out of your sandal, you know?

00:44:13.560 --> 00:44:15.414
I don't know if it's really true,

00:44:15.414 --> 00:44:18.190
but there's all kinds of
stories about Archimedes.

00:44:18.190 --> 00:44:20.950
But we do know this because
he wrote these treatises that

00:44:20.950 --> 00:44:24.180
we still have about how he
found areas of curved shapes.

00:44:24.180 --> 00:44:26.863
So one was, what's the
area of a shape like that?

00:44:26.863 --> 00:44:29.000
A so called parabolic segment.

00:44:29.000 --> 00:44:30.600
Next.

00:44:30.600 --> 00:44:31.433
This is amazing.

00:44:31.433 --> 00:44:32.370
Here's his strategy.

00:44:32.370 --> 00:44:34.500
To figure out the area
of this curved shape,

00:44:34.500 --> 00:44:36.970
he is going to regard it as a sequence

00:44:36.970 --> 00:44:39.460
of shapes made of straight lines.

00:44:39.460 --> 00:44:43.390
So, you know, in Cubist
painting you draw pictures

00:44:43.390 --> 00:44:46.820
of people or other things,
Braque and Picasso,

00:44:46.820 --> 00:44:50.133
they're using all these
rectilinear shapes.

00:44:51.120 --> 00:44:53.400
Archimedes is doing that in his vision.

00:44:53.400 --> 00:44:54.890
I think it's an amazing vision to see

00:44:54.890 --> 00:44:58.620
this curved shape as this
combination of triangles.

00:44:58.620 --> 00:45:01.990
So what he does is he puts a
big triangle inside and then

00:45:01.990 --> 00:45:05.020
there area two smaller
triangles, lighter shade of gray,

00:45:05.020 --> 00:45:07.520
sitting on the side of
the original big triangle,

00:45:07.520 --> 00:45:08.740
and then you can see there's a little bit

00:45:08.740 --> 00:45:11.060
of empty space left under the parabola.

00:45:11.060 --> 00:45:13.370
He would wedge in more triangles in there

00:45:13.370 --> 00:45:14.203
and keep doing that,

00:45:14.203 --> 00:45:16.370
and he's gonna exhaust all the area until

00:45:16.370 --> 00:45:18.830
it's all nothing but triangles.

00:45:18.830 --> 00:45:20.480
So that's his concept.

00:45:20.480 --> 00:45:23.390
But now which triangles
exactly should he put in there?

00:45:23.390 --> 00:45:24.890
Next.

00:45:24.890 --> 00:45:27.260
Oh, I should say before I get to that.

00:45:27.260 --> 00:45:28.310
Here's the big punchline.

00:45:28.310 --> 00:45:31.870
He's gonna show that the area
of that parabolic segment

00:45:31.870 --> 00:45:34.200
compared to the area of
that first big triangle,

00:45:34.200 --> 00:45:36.480
they're in the ratio four to three.

00:45:36.480 --> 00:45:39.603
And that's not obvious, but
that's what he's gonna show.

00:45:42.090 --> 00:45:45.193
The parabolic segment is 4/3
as big as the big triangle.

00:45:46.250 --> 00:45:48.830
And if you are interested
in music you should

00:45:48.830 --> 00:45:51.150
think about the numbers four and three.

00:45:51.150 --> 00:45:52.440
Do you have any musical people here?

00:45:52.440 --> 00:45:53.480
Yale is great at music.

00:45:53.480 --> 00:45:54.890
You do music?

00:45:54.890 --> 00:45:56.920
Do you, do you, but I'm
wondering, oh you do too.

00:45:56.920 --> 00:45:58.530
Wow, this is a musical family.

00:45:58.530 --> 00:45:59.940
Are you brother and sister?

00:45:59.940 --> 00:46:00.773
Yeah?

00:46:02.060 --> 00:46:04.530
Geez, but this is hard
question I'm asking.

00:46:04.530 --> 00:46:06.070
Do you know anything about why four

00:46:06.070 --> 00:46:07.760
and three are related to music?

00:46:07.760 --> 00:46:08.593
You do?

00:46:08.593 --> 00:46:09.889
- Yes.
- Why?

00:46:09.889 --> 00:46:11.340
- [Student] Isn't like the time signatures

00:46:11.340 --> 00:46:12.570
could be like three or four.

00:46:12.570 --> 00:46:14.570
- Oh, that's interesting
three, four time or something?

00:46:14.570 --> 00:46:16.073
It could be a time signature.

00:46:16.920 --> 00:46:18.296
Uh, you have a thought?

00:46:18.296 --> 00:46:22.527
- [Student] Is it the ratio
of frequencies for intervals.

00:46:22.527 --> 00:46:25.920
So, I don't, I know an
octave is two to one.

00:46:25.920 --> 00:46:27.520
- Oh good, now you're on the right track.

00:46:27.520 --> 00:46:28.540
So and octave is two to one.

00:46:28.540 --> 00:46:29.373
Tell us more what you mean.

00:46:29.373 --> 00:46:31.010
What do you mean two to one for an octave?

00:46:31.010 --> 00:46:32.623
- [Student] Um, it's that--

00:46:34.120 --> 00:46:35.300
- Like if I take two strings.

00:46:35.300 --> 00:46:36.730
Suppose I was playing a guitar.

00:46:36.730 --> 00:46:37.700
- Yeah.
- And I put my finger

00:46:37.700 --> 00:46:38.980
on a fret halfway up--

00:46:38.980 --> 00:46:40.730
- Yeah.
- The string and I pluck

00:46:40.730 --> 00:46:42.630
one string and if it was the same,

00:46:42.630 --> 00:46:44.850
the same string but with the
same tension and everything.

00:46:44.850 --> 00:46:46.170
- Yeah.
- But not with my finger

00:46:46.170 --> 00:46:48.840
on the fret, the one
that's long and the one

00:46:48.840 --> 00:46:50.980
that's half as long, they
will sound an octave apart.

00:46:50.980 --> 00:46:51.813
- Yes.
- Right?

00:46:51.813 --> 00:46:53.950
The one that's short
would be an octave higher,

00:46:53.950 --> 00:46:55.860
twice the frequency.

00:46:55.860 --> 00:46:58.670
If the frequency, if you do
this with strings but in the

00:46:58.670 --> 00:47:03.650
ratio four to three,
Pythagoras and his people,

00:47:03.650 --> 00:47:04.940
legend according to legend,

00:47:04.940 --> 00:47:06.830
discovered laws of musical harmony.

00:47:06.830 --> 00:47:08.930
The three to two ratio makes, do you know?

00:47:08.930 --> 00:47:10.070
- [Student] So it makes a third.

00:47:10.070 --> 00:47:11.890
- So three to two would be a fifth,

00:47:11.890 --> 00:47:15.323
four to three would be
called a fourth, I think.

00:47:16.520 --> 00:47:18.390
So and I don't know
music, but my daughter,

00:47:18.390 --> 00:47:21.920
who's my younger daughter
told me think about

00:47:21.920 --> 00:47:25.140
the Star Wars theme, that's a third.

00:47:25.140 --> 00:47:27.000
No, that's a...

00:47:28.240 --> 00:47:29.454
That's a fifth.

00:47:29.454 --> 00:47:30.960
(humming)

00:47:30.960 --> 00:47:33.701
That's, those are separated
by a fifth sheet too.

00:47:33.701 --> 00:47:34.833
- [Students] Yes, that's correct.

00:47:34.833 --> 00:47:36.390
- Is that right?
(audience laughing)

00:47:36.390 --> 00:47:38.774
But then she said, but
here comes the bride.

00:47:38.774 --> 00:47:40.510
(humming)

00:47:40.510 --> 00:47:41.765
I didn't do it well.

00:47:41.765 --> 00:47:43.932
(humming)

00:47:45.580 --> 00:47:46.520
That's a fourth.

00:47:46.520 --> 00:47:47.570
That's four to three.

00:47:48.460 --> 00:47:51.161
So a four to three ratio, the point, okay.

00:47:51.161 --> 00:47:52.300
(chuckling)
(audience laughing)

00:47:52.300 --> 00:47:54.030
The four to three ratio is something

00:47:54.030 --> 00:47:56.440
every self respecting Greek knew.

00:47:56.440 --> 00:47:58.360
Four to three was considered beautiful

00:47:58.360 --> 00:48:01.110
because it's connected to music and so,

00:48:01.110 --> 00:48:02.830
you could imagine how excited Archimedes

00:48:02.830 --> 00:48:04.960
would have been that the parabolic segment

00:48:04.960 --> 00:48:07.500
to the big triangle is
in a four to three ratio.

00:48:07.500 --> 00:48:08.560
Okay, but why?

00:48:08.560 --> 00:48:09.500
Why is it four to three?

00:48:09.500 --> 00:48:10.970
So let's see the argument.

00:48:10.970 --> 00:48:13.250
He takes this triangle and
now the way he constructs

00:48:13.250 --> 00:48:15.570
that triangle is he takes
the line that defines

00:48:15.570 --> 00:48:18.560
the bottom on the segment,
he slides it up until

00:48:18.560 --> 00:48:21.180
it's just touching the
parabola at one point.

00:48:21.180 --> 00:48:23.430
That's in the jargon, the tangent line.

00:48:23.430 --> 00:48:25.840
Right, it'll be tangent to
the parabola at one point.

00:48:25.840 --> 00:48:28.100
So if he, and that defines a unique point

00:48:28.100 --> 00:48:30.530
and then he builds the
triangle touching that point.

00:48:30.530 --> 00:48:33.560
Then he's gonna use that
parallel sliding trick

00:48:33.560 --> 00:48:34.650
to build new triangles.

00:48:34.650 --> 00:48:35.900
Next slide.

00:48:35.900 --> 00:48:37.250
So you see, then he builds those

00:48:37.250 --> 00:48:38.460
little triangles the same way.

00:48:38.460 --> 00:48:41.480
Slides the sides parallel
till they touch at one point.

00:48:41.480 --> 00:48:43.280
Builds new triangles and what he can prove

00:48:43.280 --> 00:48:46.170
with the geometry that
he knows and properties

00:48:46.170 --> 00:48:49.340
of the parabola that he knows,
is that those new triangles

00:48:49.340 --> 00:48:52.600
will have 1/8 the area of
the original big triangle.

00:48:52.600 --> 00:48:54.350
Not obvious, but he can prove that.

00:48:55.440 --> 00:48:58.280
And that, he shows that that
rule is true at every stage.

00:48:58.280 --> 00:49:00.710
Whenever he creates a new
triangle by this technique,

00:49:00.710 --> 00:49:02.910
it will always have 1/8 the area

00:49:02.910 --> 00:49:04.583
of the triangle it came from.

00:49:07.790 --> 00:49:09.960
And so if you total up the areas,

00:49:09.960 --> 00:49:12.340
so far we had one for the big triangle,

00:49:12.340 --> 00:49:14.450
1/8 plus 1/8 makes a quarter,

00:49:14.450 --> 00:49:17.030
so we have one plus a quarter
and then if you believe me

00:49:17.030 --> 00:49:19.550
about this rule that you're
always gonna get a quarter

00:49:19.550 --> 00:49:23.010
of what you had before,
you're led to next,

00:49:23.010 --> 00:49:24.740
an infinite series.

00:49:24.740 --> 00:49:26.280
Which is one plus a quarter plus

00:49:26.280 --> 00:49:28.530
a sixteenth plus dot, dot, dot.

00:49:28.530 --> 00:49:30.777
And that turns out to add up to 4/3.

00:49:30.777 --> 00:49:32.300
So if you've had high school algebra,

00:49:32.300 --> 00:49:34.120
you learned a formula for that,

00:49:34.120 --> 00:49:36.870
but Archimedes did not
have high school algebra,

00:49:36.870 --> 00:49:40.680
because algebra is a product
of the Middle East, right?

00:49:40.680 --> 00:49:43.060
I mean algebra is gonna be invented

00:49:43.060 --> 00:49:47.230
about like 800 years later
in places like Baghdad.

00:49:47.230 --> 00:49:50.170
I mean there's a kind of
geometric algebra that

00:49:50.170 --> 00:49:54.090
the Greeks know, but still for
them geometry is the thing.

00:49:54.090 --> 00:49:56.400
So he does his algebra this way.

00:49:56.400 --> 00:49:58.210
When he calculates this infinite series

00:49:58.210 --> 00:50:02.640
he draws a picture which has four squares.

00:50:02.640 --> 00:50:05.570
Here's a square of size one in area.

00:50:05.570 --> 00:50:08.210
The whole picture is four
units of area, right?

00:50:08.210 --> 00:50:10.410
Because it's four big squares.

00:50:10.410 --> 00:50:11.900
So that's the four in four thirds.

00:50:11.900 --> 00:50:14.400
Watch this, I don't know
if you see what's going on.

00:50:14.400 --> 00:50:16.940
You take one that's one big square,

00:50:16.940 --> 00:50:18.850
then you take as quarter of that square,

00:50:18.850 --> 00:50:21.270
that's the thing marked a quarter.

00:50:21.270 --> 00:50:22.630
Then you take a quarter of that,

00:50:22.630 --> 00:50:24.780
that's the thing marked a sixteenth,

00:50:24.780 --> 00:50:26.720
and so the total of the gray

00:50:26.720 --> 00:50:28.850
is the infinite series he wants.

00:50:28.850 --> 00:50:30.770
But he says by inspection I can see that

00:50:30.770 --> 00:50:32.623
that's one third of the whole shape.

00:50:33.890 --> 00:50:34.723
Right?

00:50:34.723 --> 00:50:35.800
Because what is it?

00:50:35.800 --> 00:50:38.240
It's a big square plus
a second sized square,

00:50:38.240 --> 00:50:41.380
plus a third sized square, but
that's also copied over here.

00:50:41.380 --> 00:50:44.940
Big square, another one of size
of quarter, the white ones.

00:50:44.940 --> 00:50:46.780
You have three copies
of the same structure

00:50:46.780 --> 00:50:48.810
in this picture of size four.

00:50:48.810 --> 00:50:51.550
So 4/3 is occupied by the gray.

00:50:51.550 --> 00:50:53.920
So that's what we would
call a proof without words,

00:50:53.920 --> 00:50:57.330
but you know what's really
nice about Archimedes here too.

00:50:57.330 --> 00:51:00.190
Now speaking to the rigorous
mathematicians for a second,

00:51:00.190 --> 00:51:03.200
is he actually calculates
the error term correctly,

00:51:03.200 --> 00:51:05.300
he doesn't just say dot, dot, dot.

00:51:05.300 --> 00:51:07.010
I mean, you're taught,
when you teach calculus

00:51:07.010 --> 00:51:08.800
properly you don't say dot, dot, dot.

00:51:08.800 --> 00:51:10.550
You say calculate the error term

00:51:10.550 --> 00:51:12.770
and make sure that the
error term goes to zero,

00:51:12.770 --> 00:51:15.440
and the error term is
that little tiny square up

00:51:15.440 --> 00:51:18.700
in the corner and he can now
see exactly how big it is.

00:51:18.700 --> 00:51:20.310
You know, in other words, he can calculate

00:51:20.310 --> 00:51:23.130
the finite series, not
just the infinite series.

00:51:23.130 --> 00:51:24.010
Okay.

00:51:24.010 --> 00:51:27.590
So anyway, that's an example
of Archimedes ingenuity

00:51:27.590 --> 00:51:30.700
with the tools he has, which
is geometry and ingenuity,

00:51:30.700 --> 00:51:31.780
but he doesn't have algebra,

00:51:31.780 --> 00:51:33.630
he doesn't even have decimals, right?

00:51:33.630 --> 00:51:36.210
Decimals are being created over in India.

00:51:36.210 --> 00:51:38.910
So this is a big world
story, the story of calculus.

00:51:38.910 --> 00:51:39.920
It's not like, don't think of it

00:51:39.920 --> 00:51:42.300
as just a European Greek thing, it's not.

00:51:42.300 --> 00:51:43.940
But this part of the story is.

00:51:43.940 --> 00:51:44.773
Okay, next.

00:51:45.870 --> 00:51:47.643
So what about Archimedes today?

00:51:49.610 --> 00:51:53.310
This is another case of this
invisible presence of calculus.

00:51:53.310 --> 00:51:56.390
You are using Archimedes
all the time when you

00:51:56.390 --> 00:52:00.740
go to the movies and possibly
when you go to the doctor,

00:52:00.740 --> 00:52:02.010
but you don't know it.

00:52:02.010 --> 00:52:03.380
So what am I talking about?

00:52:03.380 --> 00:52:05.290
You know how he built
up that curved shape,

00:52:05.290 --> 00:52:07.400
the parabolic segment out of triangles.

00:52:07.400 --> 00:52:10.320
Well you can make a shape
in computer graphics out

00:52:10.320 --> 00:52:13.380
of triangles that can
approximate any smooth surface.

00:52:13.380 --> 00:52:15.680
So you can make a picture
of a mannequins head

00:52:16.520 --> 00:52:20.390
by just triangulating it more
and more finely and that is

00:52:20.390 --> 00:52:23.963
the technology that goes into
something like the next slide.

00:52:24.820 --> 00:52:27.250
This kind of movie, Geri's Game,

00:52:27.250 --> 00:52:28.450
which you can find on YouTube.

00:52:28.450 --> 00:52:32.160
Don't do it right now, but,
so Geri's Game was the first

00:52:32.160 --> 00:52:34.817
animated movie completely
computer generated movie that

00:52:34.817 --> 00:52:38.220
had a human character that it
was emotionally expressive.

00:52:38.220 --> 00:52:40.080
We had earlier movies like Toy Story,

00:52:40.080 --> 00:52:43.370
but that was, you know, those are toys.

00:52:43.370 --> 00:52:45.970
This is a real human being
who acts like a person,

00:52:45.970 --> 00:52:47.780
except he's made of polygons.

00:52:47.780 --> 00:52:50.270
In fact, he's made of
triangles, but millions of them.

00:52:50.270 --> 00:52:52.720
So you don't see the
triangles, but they're there,

00:52:52.720 --> 00:52:56.710
and the people at Pixar
who made this, you know,

00:52:56.710 --> 00:52:58.860
animate all those polygons.

00:52:58.860 --> 00:53:00.930
But how to make a polygon that, sorry,

00:53:00.930 --> 00:53:03.040
how to make a sequence of
triangles that will make

00:53:03.040 --> 00:53:06.640
the shape that you want,
an old man's face with his

00:53:06.640 --> 00:53:08.523
wrinkles under his eyes and so on,

00:53:09.420 --> 00:53:11.210
there's a lot of calculus in computing

00:53:11.210 --> 00:53:14.250
the right triangulation
to make whatever you want.

00:53:14.250 --> 00:53:15.083
Next.

00:53:15.083 --> 00:53:18.053
So, you know, this is probably
a movie you know better.

00:53:19.780 --> 00:53:22.630
Shrek telling Donkey
that onions have layers

00:53:22.630 --> 00:53:26.530
and his little trumpet
like ears, his round belly,

00:53:26.530 --> 00:53:28.390
these are all smooth surfaces

00:53:28.390 --> 00:53:31.340
that required millions of triangles.

00:53:31.340 --> 00:53:35.456
And so this is the technology
that Pixar and Dreamworks

00:53:35.456 --> 00:53:37.410
and everybody uses nowadays.

00:53:37.410 --> 00:53:39.690
It's this Archimedean idea
that you can represent

00:53:39.690 --> 00:53:41.513
any smooth surface with triangles.

00:53:42.470 --> 00:53:43.303
Next.

00:53:43.303 --> 00:53:46.920
Now here's a little more
medically oriented example.

00:53:46.920 --> 00:53:48.520
If you just think it's kind of frivolous

00:53:48.520 --> 00:53:50.170
for kids movies, it's not.

00:53:50.170 --> 00:53:53.080
Here's a gentleman who
you can see on the left.

00:53:53.080 --> 00:53:54.873
This is him before surgery.

00:53:56.170 --> 00:53:59.200
His eyes have been, you
know, pixelated because they,

00:53:59.200 --> 00:54:02.430
for privacy, but anyway,
this guy you can see his jaw

00:54:02.430 --> 00:54:05.920
is sticking out in a
way that it's not just

00:54:05.920 --> 00:54:09.030
cosmetically unpleasing
for him, but I think it

00:54:09.030 --> 00:54:12.720
was causing him medical
problems, this malformation.

00:54:12.720 --> 00:54:15.653
And then there's a scan of his bones,

00:54:18.080 --> 00:54:19.793
taken in a medical scanner.

00:54:20.760 --> 00:54:22.070
So that's him before the surgery.

00:54:22.070 --> 00:54:25.340
Now, what doctors would
like to do is figure out

00:54:25.340 --> 00:54:28.063
if they would cut out
certain bones in his jaw,

00:54:29.970 --> 00:54:32.600
and then reattach everything
surgically, you know,

00:54:32.600 --> 00:54:34.930
sow him back up, what's his face

00:54:34.930 --> 00:54:37.190
gonna look like after surgery?

00:54:37.190 --> 00:54:39.650
And the issue is that,

00:54:39.650 --> 00:54:42.150
there's a lot of other
tissues than just the bones.

00:54:42.150 --> 00:54:44.520
Bones are pretty rigid,
but there's soft tissues,

00:54:44.520 --> 00:54:47.250
the face is made of skin,
there's all the tissues behind

00:54:47.250 --> 00:54:49.110
the face, there's cartilage,

00:54:49.110 --> 00:54:52.190
there's you know, a lot going on.

00:54:52.190 --> 00:54:54.350
Tendons, all kinds of
things to think about.

00:54:54.350 --> 00:54:58.480
So, the branch of math
that's involved here is,

00:54:58.480 --> 00:55:02.950
we want to make a mathematical
model of all the soft tissues

00:55:02.950 --> 00:55:07.950
and bones in a person's head,
such that when we change

00:55:07.960 --> 00:55:11.200
the conditions by cutting out
bones and reconnecting things,

00:55:11.200 --> 00:55:13.360
there are gonna be many elastic forces.

00:55:13.360 --> 00:55:16.740
There are gonna be parts of
the skin and cartilage and bone

00:55:16.740 --> 00:55:19.420
that are pulling on each other
with this new configuration,

00:55:19.420 --> 00:55:20.640
and things are gonna shift.

00:55:20.640 --> 00:55:23.340
Obviously that's the whole
point of doing the surgery.

00:55:23.340 --> 00:55:26.550
So, people have made
these elasticity models.

00:55:26.550 --> 00:55:28.370
Now, okay, elasticity.

00:55:28.370 --> 00:55:31.960
Elasticity is a branch of engineering

00:55:31.960 --> 00:55:34.290
and applied math that uses calculus.

00:55:34.290 --> 00:55:35.900
If you take a course in elasticity

00:55:35.900 --> 00:55:38.830
in the engineering department
at Yale or anywhere else,

00:55:38.830 --> 00:55:41.110
you will be doing tensor calculus,

00:55:41.110 --> 00:55:43.460
you'll be doing partial
differential equations.

00:55:43.460 --> 00:55:45.150
It's calculus, okay?

00:55:45.150 --> 00:55:46.590
It's been applied everywhere.

00:55:46.590 --> 00:55:50.150
And so, I'm trying to say
that when this poor guy

00:55:50.150 --> 00:55:54.020
had his face redesigned,
the doctors were able

00:55:54.020 --> 00:55:55.870
to tell him what his new
face would look like.

00:55:55.870 --> 00:55:57.270
The computer model said it would be

00:55:57.270 --> 00:55:59.103
that third image from the left.

00:56:00.140 --> 00:56:03.100
They did the surgery
and that's his new face.

00:56:03.100 --> 00:56:06.680
And you can see that the
prediction basically got it right.

00:56:06.680 --> 00:56:08.600
Now this has been quantified.

00:56:08.600 --> 00:56:09.980
I'd refer to the study in the book.

00:56:09.980 --> 00:56:12.570
You can check the original
paper if you wanna see the data.

00:56:12.570 --> 00:56:15.790
But I mean, with calculus
and computer modeling

00:56:15.790 --> 00:56:18.610
and lots of careful scanning
and all this other stuff,

00:56:18.610 --> 00:56:21.780
you can essentially build like

00:56:21.780 --> 00:56:23.980
a flight simulator for surgeons.

00:56:23.980 --> 00:56:26.250
The surgeons can do the study because

00:56:26.250 --> 00:56:28.810
you know it's very serious
to cut someone's bones.

00:56:28.810 --> 00:56:29.780
You don't wanna do it wrong.

00:56:29.780 --> 00:56:31.170
You're not gonna get a second chance.

00:56:31.170 --> 00:56:33.930
So they can practice on virtual
patients before they do the

00:56:33.930 --> 00:56:37.030
real thing, with the help of
calculus and computer modeling.

00:56:37.030 --> 00:56:38.840
So that's an example, but now what does

00:56:38.840 --> 00:56:40.380
it have to do with Archimedes?

00:56:40.380 --> 00:56:43.170
Well, when I talk about
this elasticity model,

00:56:43.170 --> 00:56:44.040
what is the model?

00:56:44.040 --> 00:56:45.610
Next slide.

00:56:45.610 --> 00:56:48.360
It's the kind of thing that
is built on triangulation,

00:56:48.360 --> 00:56:51.110
this old Archimedean idea,
except that instead of just

00:56:51.110 --> 00:56:53.810
triangulating the face, I don't
know if you can really see,

00:56:53.810 --> 00:56:56.400
but they've gone behind the
face and they have three

00:56:56.400 --> 00:57:00.400
dimensional analogs of
triangles that are tetrahedra,

00:57:00.400 --> 00:57:02.590
that are modeling the
soft tissues and they have

00:57:02.590 --> 00:57:05.140
different stiffness's
and elastic properties,

00:57:05.140 --> 00:57:07.650
and so they have this
gigantic model of billions

00:57:07.650 --> 00:57:12.250
of polygons and simplices,
tetrahedra and other things,

00:57:12.250 --> 00:57:13.890
so that they can figure out all the forces

00:57:13.890 --> 00:57:16.400
and how everything's gonna
rearrange itself after you

00:57:16.400 --> 00:57:19.410
do whatever you're gonna do
with the cutting of bones.

00:57:19.410 --> 00:57:22.860
So this is just to give
you a sense of how today,

00:57:22.860 --> 00:57:24.590
through computers and calculus,

00:57:24.590 --> 00:57:28.760
we are living Archimedes
legacy all over the place.

00:57:28.760 --> 00:57:29.653
Okay, next.

00:57:31.850 --> 00:57:34.240
All right, so I don't remember
quite when we started.

00:57:34.240 --> 00:57:35.470
I'm just about ready to wrap up.

00:57:35.470 --> 00:57:37.170
I'll give you, can I have
like five more minutes?

00:57:37.170 --> 00:57:40.220
I just wanna talk a little
bit about what you might think

00:57:40.220 --> 00:57:42.610
of as calculus, because
everything I did so far

00:57:42.610 --> 00:57:45.620
probably doesn't look
like the calculus you had.

00:57:45.620 --> 00:57:47.690
I understand that.

00:57:47.690 --> 00:57:50.210
And that's because you
know, calculus was going

00:57:50.210 --> 00:57:52.843
on for thousands of years
before algebra came along.

00:57:54.521 --> 00:57:56.470
The calculus you learn nowadays

00:57:56.470 --> 00:57:59.350
is all algebra, all formulas.

00:57:59.350 --> 00:58:02.160
It's a tremendously
powerful thing using symbols

00:58:04.090 --> 00:58:06.700
that came from the East,
that came from India

00:58:06.700 --> 00:58:10.070
and the Islamic world and
gradually made its way

00:58:10.070 --> 00:58:12.520
into Europe at the beginning
of the Middle Ages.

00:58:12.520 --> 00:58:16.910
So like 1200, 1300, you start
getting algebra in Europe.

00:58:16.910 --> 00:58:19.633
And algebra then collides with geometry,

00:58:22.051 --> 00:58:24.550
and that's when differential
calculus is soon born

00:58:24.550 --> 00:58:28.640
in the middle of you know,
like say the 1600s or so.

00:58:28.640 --> 00:58:30.640
But so 1800 years after Archimedes,

00:58:30.640 --> 00:58:34.340
algebra and geometry collide,
but before Newton and Leibniz,

00:58:34.340 --> 00:58:36.440
you have people like Fermat and Descartes

00:58:36.440 --> 00:58:39.130
creating the subject of analytic geometry.

00:58:39.130 --> 00:58:41.840
This is his fusion of taking a curve.

00:58:41.840 --> 00:58:44.190
Like here I'm showing a parabola just

00:58:44.190 --> 00:58:45.470
like Archimedes was studying,

00:58:45.470 --> 00:58:48.540
but whereas Archimedes thinks
of it as a section of a cone,

00:58:48.540 --> 00:58:52.160
now to Descartes and
Fermat, it's an equation.

00:58:52.160 --> 00:58:53.960
Like y equals x squared.

00:58:53.960 --> 00:58:56.410
The relation between these
symbols and this very familiar

00:58:56.410 --> 00:58:59.600
picture that we think
of Cartesian coordinates

00:59:00.960 --> 00:59:04.310
with x and y axis, all
the classical curves

00:59:04.310 --> 00:59:06.627
of the Greeks can now be
thought of as equations.

00:59:06.627 --> 00:59:09.870
And so you have this great, you
can play two ways with them.

00:59:09.870 --> 00:59:12.870
You can visualize 'em or you
can work on them as equations.

00:59:12.870 --> 00:59:15.020
So analytic geometry
is a great breakthrough

00:59:15.020 --> 00:59:18.910
that then sets the stage
for calculus in that.

00:59:18.910 --> 00:59:20.970
Now there's all kinds of
new curves you can make.

00:59:20.970 --> 00:59:23.130
You can write down an
equation, there's a new curve.

00:59:23.130 --> 00:59:24.720
It doesn't have to be section of a cone.

00:59:24.720 --> 00:59:25.970
You can do anything.

00:59:25.970 --> 00:59:28.270
You can create a whole
jungle of new curves

00:59:28.270 --> 00:59:29.890
and start asking questions about them.

00:59:29.890 --> 00:59:31.770
What's the area under the curve?

00:59:31.770 --> 00:59:33.500
Or we saw how Archimedes used

00:59:33.500 --> 00:59:35.693
a tangent line in his constructions.

00:59:37.750 --> 00:59:39.900
What's a tangent line gonna be like,

00:59:39.900 --> 00:59:41.860
you know, now that we're
doing it with algebra?

00:59:41.860 --> 00:59:46.097
So that became a big question
for Descartes and Fermat.

00:59:47.300 --> 00:59:49.130
And I just wanted to give you intuition

00:59:49.130 --> 00:59:51.070
about how to think about tangent lines.

00:59:51.070 --> 00:59:55.453
Imagine a microscope and I'm
gonna zoom in on this point.

00:59:56.350 --> 00:59:57.560
So let's see the next slide.

00:59:57.560 --> 01:00:00.980
If I zoom in, you don't see what looks

01:00:00.980 --> 01:00:03.723
like a parabola anymore,
but I'm now just sort of,

01:00:05.000 --> 01:00:07.970
this is the part in my
microscope field of view.

01:00:07.970 --> 01:00:09.910
The curve has gotten straighter.

01:00:09.910 --> 01:00:11.820
Sort of like what we
saw when we were doing

01:00:11.820 --> 01:00:13.927
that Archimedean pizza proof, right?

01:00:13.927 --> 01:00:16.540
And the bottom got flatter and flatter.

01:00:16.540 --> 01:00:18.470
This curve is gonna
start getting straighter.

01:00:18.470 --> 01:00:20.350
Next slide, if we zoom in more,

01:00:20.350 --> 01:00:21.990
it really looks very straight.

01:00:21.990 --> 01:00:25.840
I mean, you can see from
the numbers, .5, .505.

01:00:25.840 --> 01:00:27.410
We have zoomed in a lot.

01:00:27.410 --> 01:00:30.610
So under great magnification,
a curve starts to look

01:00:30.610 --> 01:00:33.160
like something made of straight pieces,

01:00:33.160 --> 01:00:36.350
and this is the great idea
behind differential calculus,

01:00:36.350 --> 01:00:40.230
that you can approximate curvy
things with straight things.

01:00:40.230 --> 01:00:43.170
Or what in the jargon we
would say local linearization.

01:00:43.170 --> 01:00:44.330
But it just means that you can think

01:00:44.330 --> 01:00:46.670
of a curve as made of
lots of straight pieces.

01:00:46.670 --> 01:00:50.993
But they have to be infinitesimal and um,

01:00:52.820 --> 01:00:54.920
this is again, the infinity principle.

01:00:54.920 --> 01:00:57.780
To deal a problem of a
tangent line to a curve,

01:00:57.780 --> 01:01:01.260
defined by any equation, we
can solve it by zooming in

01:01:01.260 --> 01:01:04.050
enough and then figure out
slopes of straight lines.

01:01:04.050 --> 01:01:06.720
So I wanna end with something
that is more modern.

01:01:06.720 --> 01:01:09.240
Still, you know, Fermat and Descartes,

01:01:09.240 --> 01:01:10.520
curves were just curves.

01:01:10.520 --> 01:01:12.730
They still wanted to do geometry.

01:01:12.730 --> 01:01:14.570
But nowadays we think of curves

01:01:14.570 --> 01:01:17.120
as meaningful about the world.

01:01:17.120 --> 01:01:20.110
Curves tell us about stock
prices going up and down,

01:01:20.110 --> 01:01:22.460
they show your blood
pressure, your heart rate.

01:01:22.460 --> 01:01:24.240
You know, everything can be graphed.

01:01:24.240 --> 01:01:28.160
And so for us, curves are
now visual representations

01:01:28.160 --> 01:01:30.400
of certain kinds of data.

01:01:30.400 --> 01:01:32.470
So let's see the next slide.

01:01:32.470 --> 01:01:33.830
They can represent in particular,

01:01:33.830 --> 01:01:35.370
things that are moving and changing.

01:01:35.370 --> 01:01:39.230
The dynamic world that
calculus describes began with

01:01:39.230 --> 01:01:42.050
the study of things that could
be represented as curves.

01:01:42.050 --> 01:01:44.950
So just to give you a
taste of what that's like.

01:01:44.950 --> 01:01:47.170
Let me look at the next slide.

01:01:47.170 --> 01:01:49.260
I wanna just remind you of something.

01:01:49.260 --> 01:01:51.440
So, don't start this video yet.

01:01:51.440 --> 01:01:54.750
This is a video taken in 2008.

01:01:54.750 --> 01:01:59.460
It was at the Olympics when Usain Bolt

01:01:59.460 --> 01:02:01.330
