01:04:29.280 --> 01:04:31.460
but if you look very, very closely,

01:04:31.460 --> 01:04:33.580
I can tell you that that's his shoe lace.

01:04:33.580 --> 01:04:35.161
He has an untied shoe lace.

01:04:35.161 --> 01:04:37.710
(audience laughing)

01:04:37.710 --> 01:04:41.120
So he's way, way ahead and
the question among sports

01:04:41.120 --> 01:04:44.700
aficionados was if he
had run hard, what time,

01:04:44.700 --> 01:04:47.533
he set a new world record
of I think his was 9.69,

01:04:50.330 --> 01:04:52.410
how fast could he have
run if he had really

01:04:52.410 --> 01:04:53.800
run all the way to the end?

01:04:53.800 --> 01:04:56.190
So that's a calculus question
that we could answer.

01:04:56.190 --> 01:04:58.120
So let's see the next slide.

01:04:58.120 --> 01:05:02.780
This is, the way that they
record performances like this

01:05:02.780 --> 01:05:06.740
is that there're
detectors every 10 meters.

01:05:06.740 --> 01:05:10.270
And so we know where, at what
time he crossed 10 meters,

01:05:10.270 --> 01:05:12.280
20 meters, 30 meters down the track.

01:05:12.280 --> 01:05:14.850
So we have his split
times, those are the dots.

01:05:14.850 --> 01:05:18.110
We don't have his position
in between the dots,

01:05:18.110 --> 01:05:22.040
but with calculus we can
fit a curve in an optimal

01:05:22.040 --> 01:05:25.070
way to dots and you know,
what's the smooth curve

01:05:25.070 --> 01:05:26.160
that has zero velocity.

01:05:26.160 --> 01:05:28.580
'Cause we know when he
began he wasn't running,

01:05:28.580 --> 01:05:29.490
he's in the block.

01:05:29.490 --> 01:05:31.510
You have to start with zero velocity.

01:05:31.510 --> 01:05:33.310
Zero velocity would mean this curve

01:05:33.310 --> 01:05:36.630
has to have a flat tangent.

01:05:36.630 --> 01:05:39.750
It has to, the slope tells
you how fast he's running.

01:05:39.750 --> 01:05:42.520
When it gets steeper, he's moving faster.

01:05:42.520 --> 01:05:44.560
So you can see at the beginning
he's not moving that fast,

01:05:44.560 --> 01:05:46.380
but then he gets faster and faster.

01:05:46.380 --> 01:05:48.500
Now what we could do with this having fit

01:05:48.500 --> 01:05:51.290
the curve through there,
we can then calculate

01:05:51.290 --> 01:05:53.690
the slope at each point
using what I just said.

01:05:53.690 --> 01:05:55.790
You zoom in as if with a microscope

01:05:55.790 --> 01:05:58.330
and then record the slopes at every point,

01:05:58.330 --> 01:06:01.060
and if you do that you get
the next slide which shows

01:06:01.060 --> 01:06:04.630
his velocity as a function
of time in meters per second.

01:06:04.630 --> 01:06:07.950
You see he speeds up and then
somewhere after like about

01:06:07.950 --> 01:06:11.690
eight seconds he's very
visibly slowing down,

01:06:11.690 --> 01:06:13.520
which we saw by just watching him

01:06:13.520 --> 01:06:15.220
as he's goofing around at the end.

01:06:16.416 --> 01:06:19.190
So you can answer questions
like what was his maximum speed?

01:06:19.190 --> 01:06:20.910
You can just read that off the graph.

01:06:20.910 --> 01:06:24.000
When did he achieve his
maximum speed and so on.

01:06:24.000 --> 01:06:28.140
But I haven't actually done
the calculation of how fast

01:06:28.140 --> 01:06:30.500
could he have gone if
he kept running hard,

01:06:30.500 --> 01:06:32.300
because actually we don't need to do it.

01:06:32.300 --> 01:06:34.550
The next, a few months
later there was the world

01:06:34.550 --> 01:06:38.890
championships in Berlin and
then he didn't fool around.

01:06:38.890 --> 01:06:42.950
So I don't have the video of
that, although it's on YouTube,

01:06:42.950 --> 01:06:44.270
you could watch it if you want.

01:06:44.270 --> 01:06:49.000
But people won't, thought
that something might happen

01:06:49.000 --> 01:06:52.450
in Berlin, so they went with
radar guns like the type that

01:06:52.450 --> 01:06:55.700
police would use to you know,
for catching a speeding car.

01:06:55.700 --> 01:06:59.690
So they put little reflectors
on the back of every runner,

01:06:59.690 --> 01:07:01.420
they're aiming their
lasers at the runners,

01:07:01.420 --> 01:07:04.330
and these things could
measure data, you know,

01:07:04.330 --> 01:07:07.050
like 100 times a second
or something like that.

01:07:07.050 --> 01:07:11.800
And so the next slide shows his
speed as a function of time,

01:07:11.800 --> 01:07:14.800
as detected by the radar gun at Berlin,

01:07:14.800 --> 01:07:16.230
and you'll notice some interesting thing.

01:07:16.230 --> 01:07:19.123
His instantaneous speed
is that wiggly curve,

01:07:20.090 --> 01:07:22.170
and the average that's sort of going

01:07:22.170 --> 01:07:25.530
through the wiggles is shown dotted.

01:07:25.530 --> 01:07:28.670
Do you have any idea why
there are all those wiggles?

01:07:28.670 --> 01:07:30.723
Oh, I'll take another
young, you're so fast,

01:07:30.723 --> 01:07:32.573
you should be on Jeopardy.

01:07:34.210 --> 01:07:36.907
You wanna tell, what do
you think the wiggles are?

01:07:36.907 --> 01:07:37.840
- [Student] The time it takes--

01:07:37.840 --> 01:07:38.673
- The what?

01:07:38.673 --> 01:07:40.803
- [Student] Because the time it takes

01:07:40.803 --> 01:07:42.210
for his feet to leave the ground.

01:07:42.210 --> 01:07:44.090
- Time for his feet to leave the ground.

01:07:44.090 --> 01:07:45.260
Hello.
(applauding)

01:07:45.260 --> 01:07:48.360
Yeah, you're seeing
his individual strides.

01:07:48.360 --> 01:07:50.380
You're actually seeing the fact that when,

01:07:50.380 --> 01:07:52.270
when his, I mean what is running?

01:07:52.270 --> 01:07:55.320
Running is a series of
leapings and landings, right?

01:07:55.320 --> 01:07:57.930
And when he lands he's
slowing down a little bit,

01:07:57.930 --> 01:08:00.100
and when he's leaping he's
going a little faster.

01:08:00.100 --> 01:08:02.860
So that is all being
resolved in that wiggly curve

01:08:02.860 --> 01:08:05.340
and you'll, I think there
should be 41 'cause it's known

01:08:05.340 --> 01:08:08.600
that he takes 41 steps
every time he runs the 100.

01:08:08.600 --> 01:08:11.580
Everybody else does 44, but he's tall.

01:08:11.580 --> 01:08:16.580
So he always does 41
steps, 41 strides and what

01:08:17.340 --> 01:08:19.660
I find interesting about this picture

01:08:19.660 --> 01:08:22.990
is that we don't care about the wiggles.

01:08:22.990 --> 01:08:25.110
Having measured them,
we're not interested in it,

01:08:25.110 --> 01:08:26.410
I mean they're sort of interesting.

01:08:26.410 --> 01:08:29.790
But if we really, I mean what
we really want is the trend,

01:08:29.790 --> 01:08:32.673
and I think that there's a
kinda metaphor here that,

01:08:33.820 --> 01:08:36.250
if you start getting too
precise, sometimes you're picking

01:08:36.250 --> 01:08:38.700
up information you're
not that interested in.

01:08:38.700 --> 01:08:42.010
And so, this has been a recurrent theme

01:08:42.010 --> 01:08:43.250
in the story of calculus.

01:08:43.250 --> 01:08:46.210
That like I said, it has
creative fantasies in it where

01:08:46.210 --> 01:08:49.130
it ignores certain things
and focuses on others.

01:08:49.130 --> 01:08:52.900
And it seems to me that this
is a case where you know,

01:08:52.900 --> 01:08:55.710
we could have just drawn a
smooth curve through his data

01:08:55.710 --> 01:08:57.240
and we would have gotten really just

01:08:57.240 --> 01:09:01.080
as much information as
this more microscopic view.

01:09:01.080 --> 01:09:03.670
So yes it's true, if we
start going very microscopic,

01:09:03.670 --> 01:09:06.630
calculus may break down,
but maybe we don't care.

01:09:06.630 --> 01:09:08.150
Maybe we've really gotten the essence

01:09:08.150 --> 01:09:09.880
by looking at this bigger picture.

01:09:09.880 --> 01:09:14.680
So this was said better than
I'm saying it now by Picasso.

01:09:14.680 --> 01:09:16.580
Can I have the next slide?

01:09:16.580 --> 01:09:20.500
Yeah, "Art is a lie that
makes us realize truth."

01:09:20.500 --> 01:09:23.350
Right, I mean no picture
is really realistic,

01:09:23.350 --> 01:09:26.900
and yet if it's great art,

01:09:26.900 --> 01:09:29.230
it often captures the
essence of the truth.

01:09:29.230 --> 01:09:31.890
So I would like to suggest that calculus

01:09:31.890 --> 01:09:33.770
has done the same thing for us in science.

01:09:33.770 --> 01:09:37.210
It's a kind of a lie that has
helped us realize the truth.

01:09:37.210 --> 01:09:38.207
Thank you.

01:09:38.207 --> 01:09:41.374
(audience applauding)

01:09:47.164 --> 01:09:49.247
- Thank you.
- No problem.

01:09:53.842 --> 01:09:55.890
- Thank you so much for a fantastic talk.

01:09:55.890 --> 01:09:57.288
So I hope you're gonna be open--

01:09:57.288 --> 01:09:58.575
- I'm open, I'm here.
- To take questions.

01:09:58.575 --> 01:09:59.408
Okay.

01:09:59.408 --> 01:10:02.880
- I'm happy to take any
talks or criticisms.

01:10:02.880 --> 01:10:04.253
Compliments, you name it.

01:10:05.878 --> 01:10:06.840
Uh, okay, yes.

01:10:06.840 --> 01:10:08.515
- [Student] Uh, I'd love to hear about

01:10:08.515 --> 01:10:09.800
the connection to the Declaration--

01:10:09.800 --> 01:10:11.740
- Declaration, yeah sure.

01:10:11.740 --> 01:10:13.840
That is a teaser, isn't it?

01:10:13.840 --> 01:10:15.718
Okay, here's what the story I tell

01:10:15.718 --> 01:10:18.010
in the book Infinite Powers.

01:10:18.010 --> 01:10:20.930
But, it's not my idea.

01:10:20.930 --> 01:10:23.950
This is an idea that goes back
to the historian of science

01:10:23.950 --> 01:10:27.550
I. Bernard Cohen, a great Newton scholar.

01:10:27.550 --> 01:10:32.290
So, Cohen points out that the
founding fathers in the U.S.,

01:10:32.290 --> 01:10:34.503
all you know, these
enlightenment thinkers,

01:10:36.810 --> 01:10:40.678
were very much interested
in Newton and Euclid

01:10:40.678 --> 01:10:43.240
and logical reasoning.

01:10:43.240 --> 01:10:46.430
So in particular, think
about the structure

01:10:46.430 --> 01:10:49.470
of the preamble in the Declaration, right?

01:10:49.470 --> 01:10:51.297
There's this famous ringing line,

01:10:51.297 --> 01:10:53.423
"We hold these truths to be self evident.

01:10:54.807 --> 01:10:57.410
"That all men are created
equal," et cetera.

01:10:57.410 --> 01:10:59.350
This, it's an interesting phrase.

01:10:59.350 --> 01:11:02.160
We hold these truths to be self evident.

01:11:02.160 --> 01:11:03.713
Where does that come from?

01:11:04.740 --> 01:11:09.270
If you look at Euclid, so
Euclid who gave us the first

01:11:09.270 --> 01:11:13.880
geometry textbook begins
with the self evident truths.

01:11:13.880 --> 01:11:16.030
In math we call them the axioms.

01:11:16.030 --> 01:11:17.410
All right, the axioms are the things

01:11:17.410 --> 01:11:19.240
that are supposed to be self evident.

01:11:19.240 --> 01:11:21.490
You just accept them,
of course they're true.

01:11:21.490 --> 01:11:24.060
And then starting with
the self evident truths

01:11:24.060 --> 01:11:26.770
and commonly agreed
upon rules of reasoning,

01:11:26.770 --> 01:11:30.770
you erect this edifice of
propositions and theorems,

01:11:30.770 --> 01:11:32.620
building on the axioms.

01:11:32.620 --> 01:11:34.640
Now if you look at the
rhetorical structure

01:11:34.640 --> 01:11:38.180
of the Declaration, it
has a Euclidean structure.

01:11:38.180 --> 01:11:40.520
Because now why, that is it starts we hold

01:11:40.520 --> 01:11:41.810
these truths to be self evident,

01:11:41.810 --> 01:11:45.060
then the axioms are listed,
the right to you know,

01:11:45.060 --> 01:11:46.900
pursuit of happiness and all that,

01:11:46.900 --> 01:11:49.720
and then there's an argument
and then there's an inescapable

01:11:49.720 --> 01:11:52.040
conclusion, a theorem,
which is essentially that

01:11:52.040 --> 01:11:53.870
the colonies have the right to separate

01:11:53.870 --> 01:11:57.143
themselves from the tyrant king.

01:11:59.312 --> 01:12:02.230
So, why would Jefferson have written

01:12:02.230 --> 01:12:04.390
this Euclidean document?

01:12:04.390 --> 01:12:05.800
I call it Euclidean, why?

01:12:05.800 --> 01:12:07.870
I mean 'cause rhetorically
it goes back to Euclid.

01:12:07.870 --> 01:12:11.030
But if you look at
Newton in The Principia,

01:12:11.030 --> 01:12:13.140
so Euclid is writing about geometry,

01:12:13.140 --> 01:12:15.870
but Newton is using this geometrical style

01:12:15.870 --> 01:12:17.970
of argumentation to write about the world,

01:12:17.970 --> 01:12:21.380
because the system of the
world where he begins with the,

01:12:21.380 --> 01:12:24.650
he begins with axioms, which
are his laws of motion,

01:12:24.650 --> 01:12:28.750
and then using logic
derives how comets move,

01:12:28.750 --> 01:12:31.290
how the planets move, the tides.

01:12:31.290 --> 01:12:34.210
I mean, in other words, if you
wanna make an argument that

01:12:34.210 --> 01:12:37.290
no one can argue with, to the
thinkers of the enlightenment,

01:12:37.290 --> 01:12:39.860
the best way you could do
it was to use a geometrical

01:12:39.860 --> 01:12:43.853
style self evident truths
first, theorems later.

01:12:45.900 --> 01:12:47.260
And I'm not making this up.

01:12:47.260 --> 01:12:48.990
I mean, you can see it Spinoza.

01:12:48.990 --> 01:12:53.290
So in Spinoza's ethics, the
actual title of Spinoza's

01:12:53.290 --> 01:12:57.130
ethics book is Ethics
Demonstrated in Geometrical Order.

01:12:57.130 --> 01:13:00.040
He tries to derive the rules of ethics

01:13:00.040 --> 01:13:01.300
with geometrical reasoning.

01:13:01.300 --> 01:13:03.040
That's considered irrefutable.

01:13:03.040 --> 01:13:05.267
So, I really think it's no
accident, and like I say,

01:13:05.267 --> 01:13:07.730
but I. Bernard Cohen had the idea first,

01:13:07.730 --> 01:13:09.900
that the Declaration
is this quintessential

01:13:09.900 --> 01:13:13.030
enlightenment document,
and just to top it off,

01:13:13.030 --> 01:13:17.930
we know that Jefferson had
a Newton, Euclid fetish.

01:13:17.930 --> 01:13:21.900
So, for instance, he had
a death mask of Newton.

01:13:21.900 --> 01:13:23.750
That's the kind of thing
people used to do, right?

01:13:23.750 --> 01:13:26.780
Someone is dead, you make a
big wax or I don't know what,

01:13:26.780 --> 01:13:28.540
you make some kind of
impression of their face

01:13:28.540 --> 01:13:30.881
and then it's like whoa,
cool, I have Newton's face.

01:13:30.881 --> 01:13:33.770
So, Jefferson had one of those.

01:13:33.770 --> 01:13:36.670
And he also wrote to John
Adams back when they were

01:13:36.670 --> 01:13:38.970
no longer presidents are
were just kinda shooting

01:13:38.970 --> 01:13:42.630
the breeze in 1812, I think it was.

01:13:42.630 --> 01:13:46.290
He's writing to John Adams
and he says, you know,

01:13:46.290 --> 01:13:48.990
like I'm really happy to be
done with politics and he says

01:13:48.990 --> 01:13:51.070
I've given up newspaper,

01:13:51.070 --> 01:13:54.120
I've given up reading
newspapers for Herodotus

01:13:54.120 --> 01:13:57.540
and Thucydides, Newton and Euclid,

01:13:57.540 --> 01:13:59.563
and I find myself much the happier.

01:14:00.750 --> 01:14:04.030
So, so that's the argument.

01:14:04.030 --> 01:14:07.430
But those, we also see
Jefferson using Newtonian

01:14:07.430 --> 01:14:09.940
principles like he designs a plow

01:14:09.940 --> 01:14:12.350
that will cut the soil in the optimal way.

01:14:12.350 --> 01:14:15.400
He uses a branch of math
called calculus of variations.

01:14:15.400 --> 01:14:17.960
What's the correct shape
of a plow to have minimum

01:14:17.960 --> 01:14:20.880
resistance as it's going
through the dirt and to put the,

01:14:20.880 --> 01:14:23.390
you know 'cause remember he's
interested in agriculture.

01:14:23.390 --> 01:14:25.740
To plow as much dirt as fast as you can,

01:14:25.740 --> 01:14:27.380
as easily as you can.

01:14:27.380 --> 01:14:29.710
He doesn't know calculus
of variations, Jefferson,

01:14:29.710 --> 01:14:31.830
but he knows that that's
what he needs to know,

01:14:31.830 --> 01:14:34.540
and he gets the help of a
professor at Penn to help

01:14:34.540 --> 01:14:37.600
him design this optimal plow,
which you can still see down

01:14:37.600 --> 01:14:39.920
in Monticello, is that
how you pronounce it?

01:14:39.920 --> 01:14:41.520
Cello, yeah.

01:14:41.520 --> 01:14:42.880
What, have you been there?

01:14:42.880 --> 01:14:44.143
- Monticello.
- Monticello, have you been?

01:14:44.143 --> 01:14:44.976
- In Charlottesville?

01:14:44.976 --> 01:14:47.290
- Yeah, Jefferson's old place, yeah.

01:14:47.290 --> 01:14:48.660
Yeah, yeah, it's there.

01:14:48.660 --> 01:14:49.860
Anyway, yes.

01:14:49.860 --> 01:14:51.900
So, you have a question
too or comment, no?

01:14:51.900 --> 01:14:53.760
- Oh yes, yes, is this on?

01:14:53.760 --> 01:14:55.040
