01:23:15.480 --> 01:23:19.150
because it works so
well and we didn't know

01:23:19.150 --> 01:23:21.490
that it would work that
well ahead of time.

01:23:21.490 --> 01:23:23.523
That to me it's capturing something

01:23:23.523 --> 01:23:27.260
that I wanna call the truth
or close to the truth.

01:23:27.260 --> 01:23:29.310
I wouldn't say it's the
end all be all truth,

01:23:29.310 --> 01:23:31.290
because truth is always provisional,

01:23:31.290 --> 01:23:33.220
but it's a very close to the truth,

01:23:33.220 --> 01:23:35.570
and if you wanna tell me you
don't believe there is such

01:23:35.570 --> 01:23:39.800
a thing as truth, then I
think we really do disagree.

01:23:39.800 --> 01:23:41.020
But I don't hear you saying that.

01:23:41.020 --> 01:23:43.470
I think you're saying there's
some domains where we have

01:23:43.470 --> 01:23:45.800
sort of, I almost hear you
saying the boring domains,

01:23:45.800 --> 01:23:47.100
the things we can measure.

01:23:48.494 --> 01:23:50.286
Or you're not saying that,
you don't wanna go that far?

01:23:50.286 --> 01:23:51.905
- [Student] I do
computational biology and--

01:23:51.905 --> 01:23:52.738
- Okay, okay.

01:23:52.738 --> 01:23:54.806
You're just raising the issue.

01:23:54.806 --> 01:23:57.171
- [Student] It's a little
perspective to what I'm saying,

01:23:57.171 --> 01:23:59.800
that just prove it has a quantification.

01:23:59.800 --> 01:24:02.760
- Yeah, well certainly the
things that can't be quantified

01:24:02.760 --> 01:24:04.840
I don't see what calculus
would have to say about them.

01:24:04.840 --> 01:24:06.610
Because as I say, it's all about

01:24:06.610 --> 01:24:08.730
